      "idx": 0,
      "label": "1",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The paper that invented cost-based query optimization, by the five authors named, led by Pat Selinger. This lecture re-derives the optimizer rather than describing it: every mechanism arrives as the answer to a failure we have just watched. The whole hour runs on the paper's own query, its Figure 1, over one set of table sizes that are ours, so the arithmetic is reproducible with a calculator. Continuity from the storage lectures: Get, Put, scans and indexes, in Postgres, LSM trees and B-trees, were the menu, and today is about how the system orders from it. SQL says what rows you want, not how to get them; today we reinvent the System R optimizer piece by piece to choose the how. The companion reading is Leis et al., How Good Are Query Optimizers, Really?, VLDB 2015, which checks what held up after 36 years; it is the retrospective at the front of the appendix. \n Source: §1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. This is the paper's own running example, Figure 1, and the whole hour stays on it. One change from the paper: each column in the WHERE clause carries its table's name, JOB.TITLE where the paper writes TITLE, so every predicate shows which table it touches. The boxes on the right are the three tables' columns, as Figure 1 lists them; only the join columns DNO and JOB appear in two tables. Read it aloud as English, the way the paper captions it: retrieve the name, salary, job title and department name of employees who are clerks and work for departments in Denver. Three tables. Two local predicates, each on one table: TITLE = 'CLERK' on JOB and LOC = 'DENVER' on DEPT. Two join predicates, each tying two tables together: EMP.DNO = DEPT.DNO and EMP.JOB = JOB.JOB. Point out what is missing, because it matters later: nothing in the WHERE clause filters EMP, the biggest table. Stress that the query is declarative: it fixes the answer and says nothing about the method, not which table to read first, not which index, not which join algorithm. Choosing the how is the optimizer's job, and today we reinvent the System R optimizer piece by piece. The paper's Figure 1 shows a few rows of each table and no sizes; ours follow on slides 4 and 5. \n Source: §5, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The job, as four questions, and nothing answered yet. The user did not say which table to read first or in what order to join the three; whether to use an index, and which one; which join algorithm; and, a choice that comes with the join algorithm, whether to sort. Do not answer them now: the hour answers each in turn, with its arithmetic. Say only that every one of them can change the running time by orders of magnitude on this very query, more than a hundredfold for the join order alone, and that none of them changes the answer. Then the last line, the goal: not the best plan, because the paper is explicit that its estimates are rough, but a plan close to the best, found cheaply enough to run at compile time and amortize over many runs of a compiled program. For anyone who wants the numbers now, the appendix cards from slide 68 price each question. \n Source: §1, §7. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The three tables, with sizes. The paper's Figure 1 shows three or four rows of each and no sizes at all; these sizes are ours, picked so the trade-offs show, and every number in the hour is computed from this slide, so tell the class to photograph it. Walk it top to bottom. EMP: 10,000 employees at 20 rows to a page, so 500 pages, stored in DNO order, which the next slide explains. Three indexes on EMP. DNO: clustered, 100 department numbers with 100 employees each, 50 pages. JOB: non-clustered, 50 job codes with 200 employees each, 50 pages. SAL: non-clustered, salaries from 5,000 to 25,000, 40 pages. DEPT: 100 departments on 5 pages, also in DNO order; its DNO index is clustered and unique, one row per department number, 2 pages; its LOC index is non-clustered, 10 cities with 10 departments each, 2 pages. JOB: 50 job codes on 2 pages, in JOB order; the JOB index is clustered and unique, 1 page; the TITLE index is non-clustered and unique, one job code per title, 1 page. The index sizes are assumptions, not the paper's: SAL at 40 pages, DNO and JOB at 50. Then the answer, for scale: one job title in 50 is CLERK, so 10,000 × 1/50 = 200 clerks; one city in 10 is Denver, so about one clerk in 10 works in a Denver department, about 20 rows. Slide 21 restates this table in the catalog's terms. \n Source: §5, Figure 1, extended. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two clicks: the stored order with the clustered DNO index, then the non-clustered JOB index and the takeaway. What stored in DNO order means, because the rest of the hour leans on it. EMP's rows were inserted sorted by DNO and kept that way, so one department's 100 employees sit together on 5 pages, 20 to a page; the strip shows department 50's five pages side by side. Nothing forces this. It is a choice made when the table was set up, a sensible one if employees are usually fetched by department. Then the two kinds of index. Every index lists rows in its own column's order. If the table is stored in that same order, the index is clustered and the rows it finds sit together: EMP's DNO index, top of the figure, finds department 50's 100 rows on 5 adjacent pages. If the table is stored in some other order, the index is non-clustered and the rows it finds are scattered: EMP's JOB index, bottom, finds job code 5's 200 employees spread across the table, about one page each. EMP's SAL index is the same story. A table is stored in one order, so at most one of its indexes is clustered: that is the takeaway. Last, size: an index is small next to its table, EMP's are 40 to 50 pages against 500. And the answer to our query, for scale: 200 clerks, 1 in 10 of them in a Denver department, about 20 rows. The two lookups in the figure, DNO = 50 and JOB = 5, are where rule 1 starts on slide 13. \n Source: §3: clustered indexes. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
