    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Follow the query from text to a stored plan, then run the tree exactly as written and price it."
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Name the stages and what each needs. Parse: a query block is a SELECT list, a FROM list and a WHERE tree, and a predicate may contain another block, which is the nested-query case at the front of the appendix. Catalog lookup does double duty: it checks that the tables and columns exist and what their types are, and it fetches the four statistics about the tables and their indexes that the optimizer spends the rest of the lecture using. The algebra tree is what the optimizer works on, and the loop under it is rewrite. System R does a little: conjunctive normal form, and each boolean factor pushed down to the tables it names, applied as early as possible. Later systems do a lot: view expansion, subquery flattening and unnesting. System R keeps each subquery as its own block. Choosing the physical plan gives each node an operator with an algorithm: segment scan, index scan, nested loop, merge. Code generation: System R compiled the plan to machine code; Postgres interprets; Spanner, next class, does its own thing. The stored-and-rerun loop is the economic argument for spending real CPU here: a program's plan is optimized once and run many times, and that is the paper's own framing. This is System R's pipeline, not a description of any current release. \n Source: §2, §3. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four clicks, one sign each: σ, π, ×, ⋈. The notation card, because the next slides are trees and these four signs carry all of them. Read any tree bottom-up: the leaves are stored tables, each node consumes the rows below it, and the root is the answer the user asked for. Say each sign aloud once, because students who have not seen the algebra will otherwise guess: sigma for select, which keeps rows; pi for project, which keeps columns; times for the cross product, every row paired with every row; bowtie for the join, which is a cross product with a selection on the join column, and that definition is the one worth repeating, because slide 10 is nothing but that equivalence read right to left. The predicate is written beside the sign, so sigma LOC = 'DENVER' is one node and not two. Then the sentence this lecture exists for, which is no longer printed on the slide, so say it: the four signs fix what the answer is and not one of them says how to compute it, which is why the same tree still leaves the join order, the access paths and the join methods open. The algebra is Codd 1970; Selinger's paper itself works in SQL query blocks and never draws an algebra tree (its Figures 3 to 6 are search trees of plans), so the trees on the next two slides are this lecture's, not the paper's. \n Source: Codd, CACM 13(6), 1970; §4: the WHERE tree. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
