      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four clicks, one sign each: σ, π, ×, ⋈. The notation card, because the next slides are trees and these four signs carry all of them. Read any tree bottom-up: the leaves are stored tables, each node consumes the rows below it, and the root is the answer the user asked for. Say each sign aloud once, because students who have not seen the algebra will otherwise guess: sigma for select, which keeps rows; pi for project, which keeps columns; times for the cross product, every row paired with every row; bowtie for the join, which is a cross product with a selection on the join column, and that definition is the one worth repeating, because slide 10 is nothing but that equivalence read right to left. The predicate is written beside the sign, so sigma LOC = 'DENVER' is one node and not two. Then the sentence this lecture exists for, which is no longer printed on the slide, so say it: the four signs fix what the answer is and not one of them says how to compute it, which is why the same tree still leaves the join order, the access paths and the join methods open. The algebra is Codd 1970; Selinger's paper itself works in SQL query blocks and never draws an algebra tree (its Figures 3 to 6 are search trees of plans), so the trees on the next two slides are this lecture's, not the paper's. \n Source: Codd, CACM 13(6), 1970; §4: the WHERE tree. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 12,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two clicks: the tree as parsed, then its price. This is the literal reading of FROM, WHERE and SELECT: cross product, then filter, then project. Read the tree bottom-up. The cross-product node is the literal meaning of a three-table FROM list, the selection carries all four boolean factors at once, and the projection drops the columns nobody asked for. Then the arithmetic out loud: 10,000 × 100 × 50 = 50,000,000 rows; at 20 rows per page that is 2,500,000 pages, written once and read back, so about 5,000,000 page fetches to return 20 rows from three tables that occupy 507 pages in total, 500 + 5 + 2. Say clearly that this tree is correct, which is exactly why correctness is never the optimizer's problem. For scale, the plan the optimizer will pick costs 163 page fetches, slide 53: about 31,000 times less. These are illustrative counts, not a measured result; the paper prices no such plan. \n Source: §4: the WHERE tree. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 13,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two clicks: the tree as parsed, then its price. This is the literal reading of FROM, WHERE and SELECT: cross product, then filter, then project. Read the tree bottom-up. The cross-product node is the literal meaning of a three-table FROM list, the selection carries all four boolean factors at once, and the projection drops the columns nobody asked for. Then the arithmetic out loud: 10,000 × 100 × 50 = 50,000,000 rows; at 20 rows per page that is 2,500,000 pages, written once and read back, so about 5,000,000 page fetches to return 20 rows from three tables that occupy 507 pages in total, 500 + 5 + 2. Say clearly that this tree is correct, which is exactly why correctness is never the optimizer's problem. For scale, the plan the optimizer will pick costs 163 page fetches, slide 53: about 31,000 times less. These are illustrative counts, not a measured result; the paper prices no such plan. \n Source: §4: the WHERE tree. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 14,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Compare with the previous slide: same leaves at the same coordinates, one structural change. The two local selections have moved down onto the tables whose columns they mention, TITLE = 'CLERK' onto JOB and LOC = 'DENVER' onto DEPT, and the two equality factors have been consumed by join nodes, which turns the cross product into joins. EMP gets no selection, because nothing in the query filters it: all 10,000 rows go up, and the dashed box marks the slot. The licence for all of it is the paper's own sentence, said aloud because it is not on the slide: every tuple returned to the user must satisfy every boolean factor, so each factor can be applied as early as you like. A boolean factor is one conjunct of the WHERE clause in conjunctive normal form, and it may itself be a tree headed by OR, so a factor is not the same thing as a simple comparison. Row counts on the edges: 10,000 EMP rows, 10 Denver departments, 1 clerk job code; the DNO join keeps 10,000 × 10 × 1/100 = 1,000 pairs; the JOB join keeps 1,000 × 1 × 1/50 = 20, the answer. Those fractions are selectivity factors, slide 24. System R's rewriting is small: conjunctive normal form, and each boolean factor applied as early as possible. View expansion, subquery flattening and much more came with later systems; System R keeps each subquery as its own block. Then the sting: rewriting removes the obviously stupid plans and does not tell you which of the remaining ones is good. This tree still leaves four legal join orders, four ways to read EMP and two join methods. Strawman 2 tries to choose among them with fixed rules, starting on slide 12. \n Source: §4: boolean factors; §2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 15,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Try the obvious rule book: two rules, each right on plenty of queries. Watch each one win on a simple query and break on the next, with rough counts of reads, then derive the three things the optimizer has to invent."
    },
    {
      "idx": 16,
      "label": "12",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Strawman 1 died of a rewrite; this one replaces the search with a rule book, and it deserves a fair hearing, because each rule is right on plenty of queries, which is why rule books were the serious alternative in 1979. Read the two rules as a careful programmer would write them, and give each its excuse. An index exists precisely so that a query need not read the whole table. The FROM list is right there, it costs nothing to follow, and the person who wrote it presumably knows the data. Then the counting convention, said aloud because the slide does not print it, and the four rule cards that follow, slides 13 to 16, lean on it and none of them uses a formula. Rough counts, as in strawman 1, on the tables of slide 4. A disk read brings in a whole page, so a scan reads EMP's 10,000 rows in 500 reads, twenty rows each. Rows found through a clustered index sit together, so they arrive twenty to a read; rows found through a non-clustered index are scattered, so each costs about a read of its own. The index's own pages are left out, because they are a sliver next to the data, and exact page-fetch formulas wait for the access paths, slide 30. Each rule gets the same two cards: the rule on a query where it is right, then on one where it breaks. Rough counts on this lecture's illustrative sizes, not measurements. \n Source: §4, §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 17,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The good case for rule 1, and on purpose not our query, which never filters EMP: one-table queries on EMP of the kind an application issues all day. DNO = 50 keeps one department. EMP is stored in DNO order, so its DNO index is the clustered one, and that department's 100 employees sit together on 5 pages at twenty to a page: about 5 reads, against 500 for the scan, a hundred times fewer. JOB = 5 keeps one job code, 200 rows, one in fifty. EMP is not stored in JOB order, so those 200 rows are scattered across the table and each costs about a read of its own: about 200 reads. That still beats the scan's 500, and it is the point of the second row: scattered rows win too, if they are few. This is the average case the rule was written for, and on it the rule is right. The index's own pages, a page or so per query here, are left out; the access-path section adds them back, slides 31 and 32. Rough counts, not measurements. \n Source: §3: clustered index; §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 18,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The good case for rule 1, and on purpose not our query, which never filters EMP: one-table queries on EMP of the kind an application issues all day. DNO = 50 keeps one department. EMP is stored in DNO order, so its DNO index is the clustered one, and that department's 100 employees sit together on 5 pages at twenty to a page: about 5 reads, against 500 for the scan, a hundred times fewer. JOB = 5 keeps one job code, 200 rows, one in fifty. EMP is not stored in JOB order, so those 200 rows are scattered across the table and each costs about a read of its own: about 200 reads. That still beats the scan's 500, and it is the point of the second row: scattered rows win too, if they are few. This is the average case the rule was written for, and on it the rule is right. The index's own pages, a page or so per query here, are left out; the access-path section adds them back, slides 31 and 32. Rough counts, not measurements. \n Source: §3: clustered index; §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 19,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The same rule on one more one-table query, and it breaks. SAL runs from 5,000 to 25,000, and SAL > 20000 is the top quarter of that range, so with salaries spread evenly it keeps a quarter of EMP, 2,500 rows; the selectivity section turns that into Table 1’s range formula, slide 23. EMP is stored in DNO order, so the SAL index is non-clustered, and the 2,500 rows it finds are scattered across the whole table: about 2,500 reads. The scan reads every page once and tests SAL on the way past: 500 reads. The index loses five to one. The reason is a unit mismatch, and it is the sentence to leave on the board: the index pays per row it finds, the scan pays per page. A non-clustered index wins only while the rows it finds are fewer than the table's pages, here about 500 rows, one in twenty of EMP; past that, scan. Slide 34 derives that crossing exactly. The rule would be right here too if EMP were stored in SAL order, but a table is stored in one order, and EMP's is DNO. Rough counts, not measurements. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 20,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The same rule on one more one-table query, and it breaks. SAL runs from 5,000 to 25,000, and SAL > 20000 is the top quarter of that range, so with salaries spread evenly it keeps a quarter of EMP, 2,500 rows; the selectivity section turns that into Table 1’s range formula, slide 23. EMP is stored in DNO order, so the SAL index is non-clustered, and the 2,500 rows it finds are scattered across the whole table: about 2,500 reads. The scan reads every page once and tests SAL on the way past: 500 reads. The index loses five to one. The reason is a unit mismatch, and it is the sentence to leave on the board: the index pays per row it finds, the scan pays per page. A non-clustered index wins only while the rows it finds are fewer than the table's pages, here about 500 rows, one in twenty of EMP; past that, scan. Slide 34 derives that crossing exactly. The rule would be right here too if EMP were stored in SAL order, but a table is stored in one order, and EMP's is DNO. Rough counts, not measurements. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 21,
      "label": "15",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The second rule, and the one with the best excuse: the FROM list is right there, it costs nothing to follow, and people tend to name first the table they are filtering. This query is written that way: DEPT first, filtered to Denver. A join done this way is a chain of lookups: read one table, and for each of its rows that survives its filter, find the matching rows in the other. DEPT first: LOC = 'DENVER' keeps 10 of its 100 rows, so 10 lookups into EMP, each finding one department's employees. EMP first: this query does not filter EMP, so all 10,000 rows survive and each needs a lookup into DEPT to find its department, 10,000 lookups. The LOC test can only happen once the DEPT row is in hand, so all 10,000 are paid and only 1,000 of them find a Denver department. Ten against ten thousand. The order the user wrote is the best one, and it took no search to find; that is the case rule 2 was written for. These are counts of lookups, not page fetches: what one lookup costs arrives with nested loops, slide 37. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 22,
      "label": "15",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The second rule, and the one with the best excuse: the FROM list is right there, it costs nothing to follow, and people tend to name first the table they are filtering. This query is written that way: DEPT first, filtered to Denver. A join done this way is a chain of lookups: read one table, and for each of its rows that survives its filter, find the matching rows in the other. DEPT first: LOC = 'DENVER' keeps 10 of its 100 rows, so 10 lookups into EMP, each finding one department's employees. EMP first: this query does not filter EMP, so all 10,000 rows survive and each needs a lookup into DEPT to find its department, 10,000 lookups. The LOC test can only happen once the DEPT row is in hand, so all 10,000 are paid and only 1,000 of them find a Denver department. Ten against ten thousand. The order the user wrote is the best one, and it took no search to find; that is the case rule 2 was written for. These are counts of lookups, not page fetches: what one lookup costs arrives with nested loops, slide 37. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 23,
      "label": "16",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The same rule, broken by a query written the same way. EMP is named first and EMP is filtered, so the user did exactly what the rule expects. But SAL > 20000 keeps a quarter of EMP, 2,500 rows, the same quarter as slide 14, and 2,500 surviving rows means 2,500 lookups into DEPT. Start from DEPT instead: this query does not filter DEPT, so all 100 rows survive, and 100 lookups into EMP find each department's employees, testing SAL on the way. A hundred against 2,500, and the order the user wrote is the dearer one. Being filtered is not the point; keeping the fewest rows is, and the FROM list cannot know which table that is, because it depends on the predicates and on the data, not on the sentence. Then point at our own query: FROM EMP, DEPT, JOB starts with EMP, the one table its WHERE clause does not filter at all, so rule 2 sends all 10,000 EMP rows into the first join. Two honest caveats for the note. Lookups are not all equally dear: a lookup into EMP fetches a whole department, a lookup into DEPT one row. And fewest rows first is itself only a rule: on our query it would start from JOB, which keeps a single row, and that is not the best order either, slide 52. The cost model weighs both, slide 41. Counts of lookups, not page fetches. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 24,
      "label": "16",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the query, then the table. The same rule, broken by a query written the same way. EMP is named first and EMP is filtered, so the user did exactly what the rule expects. But SAL > 20000 keeps a quarter of EMP, 2,500 rows, the same quarter as slide 14, and 2,500 surviving rows means 2,500 lookups into DEPT. Start from DEPT instead: this query does not filter DEPT, so all 100 rows survive, and 100 lookups into EMP find each department's employees, testing SAL on the way. A hundred against 2,500, and the order the user wrote is the dearer one. Being filtered is not the point; keeping the fewest rows is, and the FROM list cannot know which table that is, because it depends on the predicates and on the data, not on the sentence. Then point at our own query: FROM EMP, DEPT, JOB starts with EMP, the one table its WHERE clause does not filter at all, so rule 2 sends all 10,000 EMP rows into the first join. Two honest caveats for the note. Lookups are not all equally dear: a lookup into EMP fetches a whole department, a lookup into DEPT one row. And fewest rows first is itself only a rule: on our query it would start from JOB, which keeps a single row, and that is not the best order either, slide 52. The cost model weighs both, slide 41. Counts of lookups, not page fetches. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
