    {
      "idx": 25,
      "label": "17",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The hinge of the lecture. One rule survives both strawmen: apply filters as early as possible. That is strawman 1’s fix, pushdown, and the optimizer keeps it, because every answer row satisfies every boolean factor, so applying one early is never wrong. Strawman 2’s two rules were each right on one query and wrong on the next, and every time the deciding fact was in the data: how many rows a filter keeps, and whether the rows it keeps sit together. So stop writing rules and price the candidates: estimate the cost of each and pick the cheapest. That takes three inventions: the next part builds the first two, cost and statistics, and the search arrives once the join methods are priced, in the many-tables part. Note that cost-based optimization was not obvious in 1979; the live alternative was a better rule book. The paper's own contribution list names the expanded use of statistics, putting CPU into the cost formula (a term this lecture leaves out, slide 19), and the method of determining join order. For anyone who wants the rule book's bill on the running query, it is in the appendix, slide 75, priced once the cost model exists: the same twenty rows at 138 times the cost of the plan the optimizer picks. \n Source: §1, §7. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 26,
      "label": "18",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Cost and statistics. First the one number being minimized, page fetches, and why rows still matter to it. Then the four catalog numbers it is computed from, and our three tables in those terms."
    },
    {
      "idx": 27,
      "label": "19",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. One number per plan, so that plans can be compared, and the number is a count of page fetches. Say why in general terms before any formula. Disk is slow: reading one page from disk takes milliseconds, while the CPU work on the rows in it takes microseconds, so in 1979, and still today for data on disk, the disk dominates. And the disk moves whole pages: fetching one row costs as much as fetching the page it sits on. So count the pages a plan reads, and you have its cost closely enough to rank plans. A page fetch is one page read from disk, a data page or an index page: the same unit strawman 2 counted as reads, now made exact. Rows still matter, because they decide how many pages a plan touches; how many, for a scan and for each kind of index, is the access-path section, slides 29 to 32, with the exact formulas on slide 30. The approximate sign is honest: the paper's own formula has a second, CPU term, COST = PAGE FETCHES + W × RSI CALLS. RSI calls count the rows the storage layer passes up to the rest of the plan, and W is a machine-specific weight that turns them into page-equivalents. The paper gives W no value anywhere, and on one table the term is the same for every path anyway, because they all return the same rows. So we count pages only, and every cost for the rest of the hour is page fetches. The optional appendix slide 79 tells the rest. Then name what this objective ignores, since every later optimizer widens it: it is single-user total resource use, not latency; no parallelism, no memory pressure, no network. Spanner, next class, adds network and shard fan-out. \n Source: §4: the cost formula. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 28,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Point at the picture, not the words. Two numbers per table: rows(T) counts the lines on T's pages, the rows of T, and pages(T) counts the pages that hold them. A scan reads every one of those pages, so a scan of T costs pages(T). Two numbers per index: index_pages(I) counts the index's own pages, and distinct(I) counts the distinct keys at its leaf level. Stress that distinct is kept per index, not per column: for an index on one column we write distinct(col), and an un-indexed column has no distinct-value count at all, which is exactly where the magic constants of Table 1 come from, slide 23. There is no histogram and no sample anywhere in 1979. The paper's names are on no slide; give them only to anyone reading the paper: NCARD is rows, TCARD is pages, ICARD is distinct, NINDX is index_pages, and one that comes later, QCARD is result_rows. CARD is short for cardinality, \"how many\"; the letters before it are not consistent, the N in NCARD counts rows and the N in NINDX counts pages, which is why this deck uses plain names. The paper keeps one more number, P, which we drop: in System R several tables can be stored together in one disk segment, the area of disk a table lives in, and a scan then reads the whole segment, the other tables' pages included. Here every table has its own segment, so a scan reads exactly pages(T). Then the refresh. The numbers are set when a table is loaded or an index created, then refreshed by a manual UPDATE STATISTICS, not on every write. The paper's three reasons: the extra database operations, the locking bottleneck at the system catalogs, and the serialization of every access that modifies the relation; in one phrase, the catalog would become a hot lock. Postgres ANALYZE is the same decision for the same reasons. The consequence is that every cost in the next forty minutes is computed from numbers that may be stale, and the optimizer cannot tell. \n Source: §4: the statistics kept. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 29,
      "label": "21",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. These are the tables from slide 4, now in the catalog's terms. Tell the class to photograph this slide: every cost for the next forty minutes comes off it, and with a calculator they can reproduce the whole deck. A scan reads pages(T): 500 pages for EMP, 5 for DEPT, 2 for JOB. Two structural facts to stress. First, EMP is stored in DNO order, so its DNO index is the clustered one, and the JOB and SAL indexes cannot also be clustered, because a table has one physical order; that constraint is what makes slides 31 to 34 inevitable rather than contrived. Second, two conventions are the lecture's, not the paper's: every cost is page fetches, slide 19; and the sort estimate in the small line is one pass out and one pass back at 20 rows per page, 2 × ceil(Q / 20), because the paper gives no formula for producing a sort at all, only the cost of a merge reading a sorted inner, which is read once in total. The index sizes are assumed too: SAL 40 pages, DNO and JOB 50. Illustrative statistics, not measurements: the paper's Figure 1 gives three toy tables with three or four rows each, and no row counts, page counts or costs. \n Source: §4: statistics; Figure 1, extended. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 30,
      "label": "22",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Turn each boolean factor into a number, multiply, and then read the assumptions hiding inside the multiplication."
    },
    {
      "idx": 31,
      "label": "23",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. F is \"the expected fraction of tuples which will satisfy the predicate\", the paper's words, and its own hedge is \"very roughly\". Read the table as a design under pressure, not a lookup table: every entry is either a statistic the catalog has or a constant. Two things the abridgements usually drop. First, distinct is counted per index, not per column: an un-indexed column has no distinct count at all, so column1 = column2 needs two fallbacks, one index gives 1 / distinct on that side and no index gives 1/10. Second, the condition on column > value has two halves, the column must be arithmetic and the value must be known at access-path selection time; the second half matters precisely in System R's main use case, because a host variable in a compiled program is not known when the plan is chosen, so the 1/3 guess is used instead of interpolation. Then the paper's own ordering argument for the constants: 1/10 for an un-indexed equality is the most selective guess, 1/4 for BETWEEN is between the default selectivity factors for an equal predicate and a range predicate, 1/3 for a range has no significance to this number beyond being less selective than 1/10, and all sit under 1/2 because few queries use predicates that are satisfied by more than half the tuples. Quote the candour; it is the lesson. The caption carries the AND row, F1 × F2, because the next slides lean on it: the factors of independent predicates multiply. OR, F1 + F2 − F1 × F2, and NOT, 1 − F, are one line each on appendix slide 77. \n Source: §4, Table 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 32,
      "label": "24",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Every row count of the running query on later slides is a partial product of these four factors. Two local factors and two join factors, and nothing on EMP: the paper's query never filters EMP, so all 10,000 of its rows reach the joins, and there is no EMP row in the table. The two equality rows read straight off the catalog's distinct counts on slide 21: TITLE has 50 distinct values and LOC has 10, so 1 job code of 50 and 10 departments of 100. The max rule for the two join predicates assumes each key value in the index with fewer distinct values has a matching value in the other index; here both sides have the same count, 100 departments and 50 job codes. Keep the survivor column in view; it is the input to every join cost in the second half. Then, aloud because the slide no longer prints it, one more factor, and say clearly that it is a different query: rule 1’s one-table example, SELECT NAME FROM EMP WHERE SAL > 20000 (slide 14), which the access-path section prices with formulas. Do its interpolation on the board, (25,000 − 20,000) / (25,000 − 5,000) = 1/4, and point out that this row and the BETWEEN row are the only rows of Table 1 that look at actual key values: two numbers, a high key and a low key, with a straight line between them, which is the closest thing in 1979 to a histogram. distinct(SAL) is 2,000 and plays no part in this factor at all. Illustrative statistics, not measurements. \n Source: §4, Table 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 33,
      "label": "25",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. One bar at a time, using the factors just derived, local factors first: 50 × 10 × 100 × 50 = 2,500,000, and 50,000,000 / 2,500,000 = 20. Twenty rows is the answer size; remember it, because the plan on slide 53 has to produce exactly it. Give the concrete chain as well, because it makes the number real: 10,000 employees, 200 of them clerks, one job code in 50, and 1 in 10 of those in a Denver department: 20, the same 20 that slide 5 promised. Point out the order of the bars: the two local factors first, the filters that touch one table each, which a scan can test as it reads (search arguments, slide 29), then the two join factors. The composites the join section needs, 1,000 for EMP and DEPT and 200 for EMP and JOB, are the same kind of product over two tables rather than three: 10,000 × 10 × 1/100 = 1,000 and 10,000 × 1 × 1/50 = 200. Illustrative counts, not measurements. \n Source: §4: query cardinality. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 34,
      "label": "26",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two clicks: the question over four DEPT rows, then the answer. Independence gives 1/100 where the truth is 1/10, a tenfold underestimate, and four rows of DEPT show why. Three assumptions, named aloud because the slide does not list them, in the paper's own words: an even distribution of tuples among the index key values; note that this assumes that column values are independent; and a lack of statistics implies that the relation is small. The table carries the second. Read the four rows: every Denver department has an 802 ZIP, and Boulder, next door, has 803, so the ZIP filter keeps every row the LOC filter kept. Multiplying the two factors divides by ten twice where reality divides once: 100 × 1/10 × 1/10 = 1 department predicted, 10 in the table. Positively correlated predicates, which is what real queries carry because they are written with domain knowledge, underestimate. For uniformity, say what 1 / distinct cannot know: distinct is a single count of keys with no shape attached, so the same count is consistent with a flat salary distribution and with a lumpy one, and nothing in the catalog distinguishes them. The magic constants are the third assumption: with no index to consult, 1/10, 1/3 and 1/4 are guesses. Histograms are the eventual answer and they are not in this paper. Tell the room to hold this slide: this is the piece that aged worst, and appendix slide 62 shows how little of it changed in thirty-six years. DNO 50 to 52 and their cities are the paper's Figure 1; DNO 57 and every ZIP are illustrative. \n Source: §4, Table 1: column = value and p1 AND p2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 35,
      "label": "26",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two clicks: the question over four DEPT rows, then the answer. Independence gives 1/100 where the truth is 1/10, a tenfold underestimate, and four rows of DEPT show why. Three assumptions, named aloud because the slide does not list them, in the paper's own words: an even distribution of tuples among the index key values; note that this assumes that column values are independent; and a lack of statistics implies that the relation is small. The table carries the second. Read the four rows: every Denver department has an 802 ZIP, and Boulder, next door, has 803, so the ZIP filter keeps every row the LOC filter kept. Multiplying the two factors divides by ten twice where reality divides once: 100 × 1/10 × 1/10 = 1 department predicted, 10 in the table. Positively correlated predicates, which is what real queries carry because they are written with domain knowledge, underestimate. For uniformity, say what 1 / distinct cannot know: distinct is a single count of keys with no shape attached, so the same count is consistent with a flat salary distribution and with a lumpy one, and nothing in the catalog distinguishes them. The magic constants are the third assumption: with no index to consult, 1/10, 1/3 and 1/4 are guesses. Histograms are the eventual answer and they are not in this paper. Tell the room to hold this slide: this is the piece that aged worst, and appendix slide 62 shows how little of it changed in thirty-six years. DNO 50 to 52 and their cities are the paper's Figure 1; DNO 57 and every ZIP are illustrative. \n Source: §4, Table 1: column = value and p1 AND p2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 36,
      "label": "27",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. A map of what is left, before the first of the three parts. We can now price a plan: page fetches, from four catalog numbers and a selectivity factor per predicate. What remains is choosing, and the hour builds it up one table at a time. One table: the cheapest way to read it, a scan or one of its indexes. Two tables: the cheapest way to join a pair, by lookups or by walking both in order. Three tables, our query, and more: the cheapest order to join them in, without trying every order. Each part reuses the one before it: the join prices the ways to read each table, and the search prices joins. Do not answer any of the three now."
    },
    {
      "idx": 37,
      "label": "28",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Price one table on its own, now with Table 2’s formulas. This part contains the lecture's first hard turn, which strawman 2 showed in rough counts on slide 14: the case where the obvious index loses to a full scan. Until slide 34 the example is that one-table query, SELECT NAME FROM EMP WHERE SAL > 20000; the running query comes back with the joins, slide 36."
    },
    {
      "idx": 38,
      "label": "29",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the page strip alone, then the segment scan, then the index scan. Say first which query is on the screen: not the running query, whose EMP has no predicate at all, but rule 1’s one-table query, SELECT NAME FROM EMP WHERE SAL > 20000, which stays the example through slide 34. Two panels, one shared page strip. A segment scan, the paper's name for a full table scan, sweeps every page of the table once and produces no order. An index scan walks the leaf level in key order, optionally between a start key and a stop key, and produces rows in that order; remember that for slide 46. Then the term the rest of the lecture leans on, said aloud because it is not on the slide: a filter of the form column op value is sargable, a search argument. Both scans test it as they read, so a failing row is dropped on the spot at no extra page cost: the scan reads the same pages whether the row passes or not. The running query has one on each of DEPT and JOB: a scan of DEPT reads its 5 pages, tests LOC = 'DENVER' on all 100 rows, and keeps 10, for 5 page fetches. A comparison of two columns of the same table is not sargable; it is tested only after the row has been read. That is predicate pushdown, in 1979: reject rows where they are read, as early and as cheaply as possible. An index whose key starts with the column can do more than test the filter: it can use it to start and stop its range. Here SAL > 20000 becomes a start key of 20,000 and no stop key: the scan begins at the first entry above 20,000 and runs to the end of the index. A matching predicate narrows the index range and the number of rows fetched, which is why F multiplies the whole bracket in Table 2 rather than one term of it. Define a matching index here, in the paper's words: a set of predicates matches an index access path when the predicates are sargable and the columns they mention are an initial substring of the index key. The paper's own example is a NAME, LOCATION index matching NAME = SMITH AND LOCATION = SAN JOSE; LOCATION = SAN JOSE alone matches nothing, because it skips the first key column and the San Jose entries are scattered through the index. A non-matching predicate can still ride along as a search argument; it just does not narrow the scan. \n Source: §3: scans and search arguments; §4: matching index. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
