      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the page strip alone, then the segment scan, then the index scan. Say first which query is on the screen: not the running query, whose EMP has no predicate at all, but rule 1’s one-table query, SELECT NAME FROM EMP WHERE SAL > 20000, which stays the example through slide 34. Two panels, one shared page strip. A segment scan, the paper's name for a full table scan, sweeps every page of the table once and produces no order. An index scan walks the leaf level in key order, optionally between a start key and a stop key, and produces rows in that order; remember that for slide 46. Then the term the rest of the lecture leans on, said aloud because it is not on the slide: a filter of the form column op value is sargable, a search argument. Both scans test it as they read, so a failing row is dropped on the spot at no extra page cost: the scan reads the same pages whether the row passes or not. The running query has one on each of DEPT and JOB: a scan of DEPT reads its 5 pages, tests LOC = 'DENVER' on all 100 rows, and keeps 10, for 5 page fetches. A comparison of two columns of the same table is not sargable; it is tested only after the row has been read. That is predicate pushdown, in 1979: reject rows where they are read, as early and as cheaply as possible. An index whose key starts with the column can do more than test the filter: it can use it to start and stop its range. Here SAL > 20000 becomes a start key of 20,000 and no stop key: the scan begins at the first entry above 20,000 and runs to the end of the index. A matching predicate narrows the index range and the number of rows fetched, which is why F multiplies the whole bracket in Table 2 rather than one term of it. Define a matching index here, in the paper's words: a set of predicates matches an index access path when the predicates are sargable and the columns they mention are an initial substring of the index key. The paper's own example is a NAME, LOCATION index matching NAME = SMITH AND LOCATION = SAN JOSE; LOCATION = SAN JOSE alone matches nothing, because it skips the first key column and the San Jose entries are scattered through the index. A non-matching predicate can still ride along as a search argument; it just does not narrow the scan. \n Source: §3: scans and search arguments; §4: matching index. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 41,
      "label": "30",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Six situations and eight formulas: both non-clustered rows have a second, buffer-fit form, pages in place of rows, which the caption mentions and appendix slide 78 draws. Read row one as the degenerate case: a unique index on an equality returns at most one row, so one index page plus one data page, and no statistic is needed at all; JOB's unique TITLE index is that row, 1 + 1 = 2. Rows four and five are the full index scan, where no boolean factor matches, so the F multiplier disappears and the path reads the whole index and the whole table. Those two rows are usually dropped from abridgements and they are load-bearing here, because in the running query EMP has no predicate at all, so every one of EMP's index paths matches nothing: its DNO index reads 50 + 500 = 550 and its JOB index 50 + 10,000 = 10,050, appendix slide 80. The last row is the segment scan, every page of the table once: pages, 500 for EMP. Then set up the next three slides. Ask the room to stare at rows two and three, which are character-for-character identical except for pages against rows. For EMP that is 500 against 10,000, a factor of twenty hiding in one word. Strawman 2 said the same thing in rough counts, 20 rows to a read against about a read per row (slides 13 and 14); the next two slides draw it. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 42,
      "label": "31",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The first of a matched pair: tell the class the next slide is this same picture with exactly one thing changed, so they should memorise the geometry now. Still the one-table query, SAL > 20000. The index leaves are in key order and the data pages are in the same order, so the qualifying range is a contiguous run of pages and each page is fetched once no matter how many qualifying rows sit on it. The paper's definition is worth quoting: for a clustered index, not only each index page but also each data page containing a tuple from that relation will be touched only once in a scan on that index. Table 2’s clustered-matching row is that sentence as arithmetic: F × (index_pages + pages) = one quarter of (40 + 500) = 135, which is 10 index pages and 125 data pages. Be explicit that this panel is a counterfactual, because EMP is stored in DNO order, not SAL order, and that is the next slide's point. Illustrative layout, not a measured trace. \n Source: §3: clustered index; §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 43,
      "label": "32",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The matched partner, and nothing has moved: the same leaf strip, the same page strip, the same 2,500 qualifying rows. The arrows changed. The index is ordered by SAL while the pages are ordered by DNO, so consecutive index entries point at unrelated pages, and the page highlighted in the figure is fetched five separate times. The paper states the assumption behind charging for every one of those: for non-clustered indexes it is assumed that for those relations not fitting in the buffer, the relation is sufficiently large with respect to the buffer size that a page fetch is required for every tuple retrieval. That assumption, not the B-tree, is what turns pages into rows in the formula: F × (index_pages + rows) = one quarter of (40 + 10,000) = 2,510. This is strawman 2’s about a read per row, slides 13 and 14, with a formula under it: the 2,500 reads it estimated, plus the index's own 10 pages. Then the structural point, which is the whole reason this example works: EMP is stored in DNO order, a table has at most one physical order, so the SAL index has to be the non-clustered one. This is forced, not unlucky. Illustrative page numbers, not a measured trace. \n Source: §3; §4, Table 2 and the buffer-pool paragraph. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 44,
      "label": "33",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The slide the lecture turns on, and the one to slow down for. Three bars, three Table 2 rows, one predicate of the one-table query, the same 2,500 rows returned by every path. The non-clustered index at 2,510 is what rule 1, always use an index if one exists, picks; the segment scan at 500 wins by 5.02 times; the same index, had it been clustered, would have cost 135 and won by 3.7 times. Strawman 2 reached the same verdict from rough counts, about 2,500 reads against 500 (slide 14); now Table 2 stands under it. Every path tests SAL > 20000 as a search argument, so every path returns the same 2,500 rows; what differs is how many pages each touches to find them, and pages are all the cost counts (slide 19). Say it in one line, since the slide no longer prints it: the obvious index loses to a plain scan by 5×, because the scan pays per page, the non-clustered index per row, the clustered one per page of matching rows. Answer the review question directly: the two statistics that tell you the index is wrong are pages and rows. EMP's other two paths for this query, the DNO and JOB indexes matching nothing at 550 and 10,050, are on appendix slide 72. The paper gives no worked single-table cost arithmetic anywhere; its Figures 2 to 4 are symbolic and prune paths by assumption. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 45,
      "label": "34",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Generalise the previous slide into something reusable, still on the one-table query. The index line passes through the origin with a slope of about one fetch per qualifying row, 1.004 to be exact; at 100 rows it costs 100.4. The scan line is flat at 500. They cross where F × (index_pages + rows) = pages, which is F = 500 / 10,040, about five per cent, roughly 498 rows, just under the 500-page count because the index's own 40 pages tip it. Give the class the sentence to write down: the index loses once the answer has more rows than the table has pages. That is where strawman 2’s past about 500 scattered rows, scan came from (slide 14): the 500 is pages(EMP). Then say what moves the crossing, because that is the deeper point: more rows per page moves it left, because the scan gets cheaper while the index still pays per row; a clustered index removes it almost entirely; and a buffer large enough to hold the fetched pages removes it too, which is appendix slide 78. All three are properties of the data and the physical design, which is why no rule book can encode the threshold, and why the optimizer can only notice clustering, never create it. Our arithmetic, not the paper's. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 46,
      "label": "35",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Two algorithms and one cost shape, so the whole contest is over what one pass of the inner costs. One is an old acquaintance from strawman 2, rule 2’s lookups; the other, the merge join, is defined here, in words and then step by step, before it is priced."
    },
    {
      "idx": 47,
      "label": "36",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Open on the loop, the plainest way to write a join: for every DEPT row, look at every EMP row, and emit the pairs that agree on DNO. That is what joining EMP and DEPT on DNO means, and every join method is a cheaper way to produce the same pairs. Then the two words the rest of the hour uses, one per box. The outer is the table of the outside loop: it is read once, row by row. The inner is the table of the inside loop: it is read again for every outer row, so whatever one pass of the inner costs is paid once per outer row; one lookup into the inner is called a probe, the thing strawman 2 called a lookup. Say explicitly that outer and inner are the outside and inside of a loop and have nothing to do with an outer join, which is a different thing and not in this paper. Written this way the loop is hopeless, 100 × 10,000 = a million pairs examined to keep 10,000, and only 1,000 of those are in Denver; the next slide makes it cheap twice over: the Denver filter runs on the outer before the inner loop starts, and the inner loop becomes a lookup through EMP's index. \n Source: §5: the outer relation and the inner relation. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 48,
      "label": "37",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the loop, then what line two really is and one probe's price, then the formula. The first method, written out, because an audience that has not met it will not get it from a cost formula. These are rule 2’s lookups from slide 15, now with a price on each one. Read the three lines aloud as English: take one Denver department, find that department's employees, emit each pair, repeat. Then say what is not in the pseudocode and matters most: the inner line is not a scan of EMP, it is an access path chosen by exactly the Table 2 machinery of slide 30, with the outer row's DNO supplied as the constant. That is how a join predicate that is not sargable on its own becomes sargable once an outer row provides a value, and therefore how the inner index gets to match. Derive the probe on the board: EMP through its clustered DNO index, F = 1/distinct(DNO) = 1/100, costs 1/100 × (50 + 500) = 5.5 pages, half an index page and the 5 data pages that hold one department's 100 employees. Ten probes cost 55, and the whole plan 5 + 55 = 60. Then generalise to the formula, and point out the asymmetry it hides: the outer is paid once and the inner outer_rows times, so the two tables are not interchangeable even though the answer is. Every join cost from here on is page fetches, the measure of slide 19. \n Source: §5: nested loop join. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
