    {
      "idx": 51,
      "label": "38",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The same three lines as pages rather than rows, which is where the cost comes from. Walk one red outer row through the figure and then the repeat label on the arrow, and be explicit that the arrow is the whole argument of the next section: everything at its far end is paid outer_rows times. The inner's 5.5 pages are one probe of EMP's clustered DNO index, 1/100 × (50 + 500), and the index is matching because the outer row supplied a DNO value. Then the line under the figure: ask what happens if the inner has no index on the join column, and answer it. The inner pass becomes a scan of all 500 pages, and ten probes become 10 × 500 = 5,000 instead of 55. Then turn the loop around: with EMP outside, every one of its 10,000 rows looks DEPT up through DEPT's unique index at 2 pages, 500 + 10,000 × 2 = 20,500, which slide 41 prices next to 60. Illustrative layout, not a measured trace. \n Source: §5: nested loop join. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 52,
      "label": "39",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The second join method, before it is priced. Nested loops are rule 2’s lookups: for each row of one table, find its matches in the other. A merge join is the other way to join two tables. Put both in join-column order, then walk them side by side, the way you merge two sorted lists in a first programming course: compare the two current rows, output on a match, step the side that is behind. Each table is read once, start to finish, and there are no lookups at all. The price is the first step: both tables must be in that order before the walk starts, and a table that is not has to be sorted, or read through an index in that order. So when is it worth it? When the lookups would be many. Rule 2’s query with EMP first needed 10,000 lookups into DEPT, and one walk instead of ten thousand lookups is a bargain. DEPT first needed 10, and ten lookups are cheaper than putting anything in order. System R had exactly these two join methods; slides 37 and 41 price both. Then answer why only two: Blasgen and Eswaran, 1976, found one of the two near-optimal except for very small relations, so System R implemented two and stopped. Quote the paper's own sentence, the cleanest in Section 5: the two cost formulas are essentially the same, and the reason that merging scans is sometimes better than nested loops is that the cost of the inner scan may be much less. Hash join is a 1980s addition, GRACE 1983 and hybrid hash, DeWitt et al. 1984, and today's engines have all three; appendix slide 84 has the dates. \n Source: §5: merging scans. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 53,
      "label": "40",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Five clicks: both cursors at the top, then one per step of the trace. One merge, step by step, on the paper's own Figure 1 rows and on the pair the nested loop of slide 38 and the comparison of slide 41 both use: DEPT with EMP on DNO. LOC = 'DENVER' has already run on DEPT, which leaves 50 MFG and 52 SHIPPING; 51 BILLING is in Boulder. Neither side needs sorting: DEPT is stored in DNO order, and so is EMP, SMITH 50, JONES 50, DOE 51. Both cursors start at the top; click once per step. One: 50 MFG against SMITH 50, equal, output the pair, and step EMP only. Two: the same 50 against JONES 50, equal again, output it. That is why a match steps only EMP: DNO is unique in DEPT, but the next EMP row may share it, as JONES shares SMITH's 50. Three: 50 against DOE 51. 50 is smaller, so DEPT steps: both lists are sorted, so nothing later in EMP can be a 50. Four: 52 SHIPPING against DOE 51. Now 51 is smaller, so EMP steps, and it has no rows left, so the merge is done: DOE, whose department is in Boulder, is passed over without a single lookup. The two stepping rules are said aloud, not printed: the smaller side steps forward, because nothing later on the other side can match it; and on a match only EMP moves on, because the next EMP row may share the key. Count what was read: each DEPT row once, each EMP row once, two pairs out; in the running query TITLE = 'CLERK' then keeps only JONES, the clerk. Then the two cost lines at the bottom, with this pair's numbers said aloud: a merge reads each input once, in join-column order. Here both arrive in DNO order through their clustered indexes, EMP for 50 index pages + 500 table pages and DEPT for 2 + 5, so the merge costs 557, the fourth row of slide 41. Had EMP been read by a scan, it would need a sort first, 500 + 2 × ceil(10,000 / 20) = 1,500, and the merge would cost 1,507, the route slide 46 draws. A non-clustered index in the right order costs about a page per row, EMP by JOB 50 + 10,000, so it rarely beats the sort; and a composite from an earlier join has no table pages to read, only what built it plus a sort if needed. \n Source: §5: merging scans; Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
