    {
      "idx": 58,
      "label": "41",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Both methods are now on the table, so price them, and notice that rows one and three are rule 2’s two orders from slide 15, 10 lookups or 10,000, with real prices on them. Four candidates from slide 21 and slide 24 alone. The 342-fold gap between rows one and three, 20,500 against 60, comes from outer_rows, since the inner cost is paid once per outer row: 10 lookups at 5.5 against 10,000 lookups at 2. Same two tables, same two indexes, 60 or 20,500; say it aloud, since the slide shows only the table. EMP's lookup into DEPT is even the cheaper one, 2 pages against 5.5, and it does not come close to helping. Same join, same answer, same two indexes. Row two is two pages dearer and arrives in DNO order, so the search keeps both, a first sight of the keep rule slide 46 states. Row four is the merge, and it loses here for the reason slide 39 gave: it reads all of EMP in DNO order, 550 by itself, to save ten lookups that cost 55. Say that the merge's vindication is coming on slide 52, where the composite has 1,000 rows and a sort plus one pass of JOB, 100 + 3 = 103, replaces 1,000 lookups at 2 pages, 2,000. Three further candidates for this pair, 1,507, 20,550 and 30,050, are in appendix slide 81. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing the cost of joins, Figure 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 59,
      "label": "42",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. The next slide states the problem and counts the candidates. This part contains the lecture's second hard turn: dynamic programming over subsets."
    },
    {
      "idx": 60,
      "label": "43",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The problem this part solves, stated before anything clever. A join's result does not depend on the order the tables are joined in; its cost depends on it hugely, 163 or 20,603 page fetches for our three tables, the first question on slide 3. The naive search is the obvious one: list every order, price each, keep the cheapest. With n tables that is n! orders, and each order also picks a join method for each of its n − 1 joins and an access path for each table, which multiplies the count again, so the table understates the work. Factorial means the n-th table multiplies the work by n. The right-hand column is search time, not the plan's page fetches; read it at a deliberately generous microsecond to price a whole order: nothing at three or eight tables, eight minutes at twelve, 77,000 years at twenty; fifteen tables, not on the slide, would take two weeks. Real queries get there: the benchmark Leis built averages eight joins, nine tables, and its largest query joins seventeen tables, about eleven years at this rate. The next four slides find the observation that replaces orders with subsets, and slide 48 counts what it saves. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 61,
      "label": "44",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the question, then the continuation and the answer, then the order caveat. The paper's sentence in full: the method to join the composite to the k+1-st relation is independent of the order of joining the first k. Go slowly; this is the most important slide in the second half. DEPT joined to EMP at 60 and EMP joined to DEPT at 20,500 are different plans with wildly different costs, but they produce the same 1,000 rows, with the same columns, with the same two predicates already applied, LOC = 'DENVER' and EMP.DNO = DEPT.DNO. So the problem of adding JOB is the identical function of both, and the cost of adding JOB is the identical number: the same predicates apply, the same orders are useful, the same join methods. Therefore the history of a composite is irrelevant except through three facts: which tables are in it, how many rows it has, and what order they are in. Take a show of hands before revealing; the usual wrong answer is that the shape of the subtree must matter. The first click adds the continuation in red, priced once off the state box. The second click draws the caveat in the figure's lower branch, because it is where intuition breaks and the next two slides live: if the two sub-plans differ in order, the merge costs differ, 3 pages if the composite already arrives in JOB order and 100 + 3 if it must be sorted first, so they are not interchangeable. Nested loops into JOB cost 1,000 × 2 = 2,000 in any order. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 62,
      "label": "44",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the question, then the continuation and the answer, then the order caveat. The paper's sentence in full: the method to join the composite to the k+1-st relation is independent of the order of joining the first k. Go slowly; this is the most important slide in the second half. DEPT joined to EMP at 60 and EMP joined to DEPT at 20,500 are different plans with wildly different costs, but they produce the same 1,000 rows, with the same columns, with the same two predicates already applied, LOC = 'DENVER' and EMP.DNO = DEPT.DNO. So the problem of adding JOB is the identical function of both, and the cost of adding JOB is the identical number: the same predicates apply, the same orders are useful, the same join methods. Therefore the history of a composite is irrelevant except through three facts: which tables are in it, how many rows it has, and what order they are in. Take a show of hands before revealing; the usual wrong answer is that the shape of the subtree must matter. The first click adds the continuation in red, priced once off the state box. The second click draws the caveat in the figure's lower branch, because it is where intuition breaks and the next two slides live: if the two sub-plans differ in order, the merge costs differ, 3 pages if the composite already arrives in JOB order and 100 + 3 if it must be sorted first, so they are not interchangeable. Nested loops into JOB cost 1,000 × 2 = 2,000 in any order. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 63,
      "label": "44",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the question, then the continuation and the answer, then the order caveat. The paper's sentence in full: the method to join the composite to the k+1-st relation is independent of the order of joining the first k. Go slowly; this is the most important slide in the second half. DEPT joined to EMP at 60 and EMP joined to DEPT at 20,500 are different plans with wildly different costs, but they produce the same 1,000 rows, with the same columns, with the same two predicates already applied, LOC = 'DENVER' and EMP.DNO = DEPT.DNO. So the problem of adding JOB is the identical function of both, and the cost of adding JOB is the identical number: the same predicates apply, the same orders are useful, the same join methods. Therefore the history of a composite is irrelevant except through three facts: which tables are in it, how many rows it has, and what order they are in. Take a show of hands before revealing; the usual wrong answer is that the shape of the subtree must matter. The first click adds the continuation in red, priced once off the state box. The second click draws the caveat in the figure's lower branch, because it is where intuition breaks and the next two slides live: if the two sub-plans differ in order, the merge costs differ, 3 pages if the composite already arrives in JOB order and 100 + 3 if it must be sorted first, so they are not interchangeable. Nested loops into JOB cost 1,000 × 2 = 2,000 in any order. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
