    {
      "idx": 64,
      "label": "45",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The correctness argument, in numbers rather than in the abstract. Set it up aloud, since the slide shows only the table: suppose the best three-table plan used the 20,500 plan for {EMP, DEPT}, and swap in the 60. The continuations' prices: JOB.JOB is unique, so one probe is 1 + 1 = 2; one pass of JOB in JOB order is its clustered index, 1 + 2 = 3; sorting the 1,000-row composite costs 2 × ceil(1,000 / 20) = 100. Row three is the argument and rows one and two are its instances: because the continuation only consumes 1,000 rows with the same columns, its cost X is the same number in both columns, so the difference between the two totals is always exactly 20,500 − 60 = 20,440, whatever sits above. An optimal plan containing the 20,500 subplan could therefore always be improved, which means no optimal plan contains it, so discarding it loses nothing. That is the exchange argument, and it is what licenses keeping one plan per subset instead of one per order. Two of the four totals are old friends: 22,500 in row one is exactly the rule book's plan, EMP scanned, DEPT probed, JOB probed, which appendix slide 75 prices, and 20,603 in row two is the FROM order run as written, EMP outside with DEPT found by lookups, then a sort and merge for JOB. The swap turns both into plans built on the 60. Then state the repair the next slide pays for: the swap is only legal while the cheaper plan delivers everything the parent needed, and order is the one thing it can fail to deliver. Sort cost is our own one-pass estimate, 2 × ceil(1,000 / 20) = 100 at 20 rows per page. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 65,
      "label": "46",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: two of EMP's four paths, the 500 scan the cheaper on its own; then what keeping only the scan costs at the merge, a 1,000-page sort, 1,507; then the kept ordered path, 557, and the takeaway. The slide that says why the search keeps more than one plan per table, at the exact point where the optimality argument needs it. Say it in plain words first, as the top lines do: the scan is the cheapest way to read EMP, but the DNO index returns rows already in DNO order, and a later join may want that order, so the search keeps both. The paper's name for such an order is an interesting order, one that a join, ORDER BY or GROUP BY can use; the slides say useful order and do not define the term. Its pruning rule is to examine only the cheapest access path which produces tuples in each interesting order and the cheapest unordered access path. Our interesting orders are DNO and JOB, the two join columns, because the merge join of slide 39 walks both inputs in join-column order, so a plan that already delivers that order can save a sort later. For EMP that keeps three of its four paths: the 500 scan, the 550 DNO index and the 10,050 JOB index, twenty times the scan and kept only for its order; the SAL index, 10,040, is pruned, in an order nobody wants; appendix slide 80 has every path. In the paper's model a segment scan has no order, so EMP's DNO order always comes through its clustered index. If the state were just the cheapest plan for this subset, the 550 plan would have been discarded at the first level, and the merge option for EMP and DEPT would have had to start from the 500 scan and pay 1,000 to sort all 10,000 rows, 2 × ceil(10,000 / 20), 500 pages out and 500 back: 500 + 1,000 + 7 = 1,507 instead of 550 + 7 = 557. Fifty extra pages for the ordered path save 950 at the merge. The algorithm would still be correct for the plans it kept; it would simply have lost a good one from the space it searched. Hence the state is a pair, subset and order, and the table has seven entries at level one rather than three. Say the caveat out loud rather than hiding it: on this instance the merge loses to the 60 nested loop anyway, so nothing about the final plan changes. The point is the pruning rule, and a student who notices should be told they are right. One sentence on equivalence classes: EMP.DNO and DEPT.DNO are the same order once the two are joined, so one entry covers both, which is appendix slide 83. Sort cost is our own one-pass estimate at 20 rows per page; the paper gives no sort-production formula. \n Source: §4: interesting orders; §5: one solution per order equivalence class. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
