    },
    {
      "idx": 68,
      "label": "47",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The algorithm the last three slides licensed, written the way the notes write it. Four clicks: one per level of the loop, then everything under it at once. Read it level by level. Level one is slide 46's keep rule applied to every table: the cheapest path, plus the cheapest in each useful order, the paper's interesting orders. Level two extends each level-one survivor by one more table, which is always the inner, priced with both join methods, and keeps the cheapest for each pair and each order; level three does the same from the pairs, and so on up to all n tables. Nothing at level k + 1 looks at anything but level k's survivors, which is exactly what the observation and the exchange argument made safe, and the order tag is what slide 46 added. The paper builds the same table by iterating on the number of relations joined so far. On our query the levels hold 3 singles, 2 pairs and 1 triple, and the lattice on slide 51 is this loop run by hand. \n Source: §5: computing join order; the search tree. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
