    },
    {
      "idx": 72,
      "label": "48",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The orders column is slide 43's; the new column is subsets. Make the room read the n = 3 row and notice that the clever algorithm is worse on our own running example; say the one-line summary aloud, exponential instead of factorial, and at three tables a loss. The column counts subsets, not plans priced: our query enters 6 of its 7 non-empty subsets. Honesty buys trust, and it tells them where the technique is for: we are doing this to learn it, not because a three-table query needs it. The crossover is the real content, pointless on toy queries and decisive on real ones, and Leis's queries average eight joins. Then the paper's own bound: the number of solutions stored is at most 2 to the n times the number of interesting result orders, with the time to build the tree roughly proportional to the same number. Left-deep enumeration prices about n × 2 to the n−1 joins, so 1,024 for eight tables. Our 30 costs break down as 10 single-table paths, 14 pair plans and 6 triple plans; appendix slides 80 and 81 list all of them. \n Source: §5: at most 2n solutions. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 73,
      "label": "49",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the six orders, then the two that open with a cross product struck. The first of two heuristics on top of the subset search, and a property of the query, readable off the join graph before any cost is computed. EMP to DEPT and EMP to JOB are edges; DEPT to JOB is not. So two of the six orders on the right die, DEPT then JOB then EMP and JOB then DEPT then EMP, because each would open by pairing every DEPT row with every JOB row, a cross product, and one lattice node, {DEPT, JOB}, is never priced. The four survivors are the four legal orders, priced in full on appendix slide 68. A cross product is unavoidable only when the join graph is disconnected, and then the job is to take it as late as possible. \n Source: §5: Cartesian products postponed. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 74,
      "label": "49",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two clicks: the six orders, then the two that open with a cross product struck. The first of two heuristics on top of the subset search, and a property of the query, readable off the join graph before any cost is computed. EMP to DEPT and EMP to JOB are edges; DEPT to JOB is not. So two of the six orders on the right die, DEPT then JOB then EMP and JOB then DEPT then EMP, because each would open by pairing every DEPT row with every JOB row, a cross product, and one lattice node, {DEPT, JOB}, is never priced. The four survivors are the four legal orders, priced in full on appendix slide 68. A cross product is unavoidable only when the join graph is disconnected, and then the job is to take it as late as possible. \n Source: §5: Cartesian products postponed. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 75,
      "label": "50",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The second heuristic, on its own slide because it is the one 1979 restriction later systems dropped. Use slide 36's words: every join has an outer, read once, and an inner, consulted again for each outer row. Left-deep means the inner is always one base table, so each join adds one table to the composite built so far, and a plan's tree is fully described by its order, A, B, C, D on the left. The bushy tree on the right joins two composites, A ⋈ B and C ⋈ D, so its top join has a composite as its inner; System R never prices that shape. A bushy tree needs at least four tables, so our three-table query cannot have one; what the rule rules out here is the {DEPT, EMP} pair on the inside, JOB ⋈ (DEPT ⋈ EMP), the same rows at a different cost, which is the caption. Why accept the restriction: a base-table inner can be reached by an index probe, which is what made slide 37's 5.5-page probe available at every step; a composite is only ever the outer, so it can pipeline into the next join and is stored only if that join must sort it, as the winning plan's 1,000-row composite is on slide 53, while a composite inner, re-read once per outer row, would have to be stored; and the space shrinks to orders of tables, which the subset search then prices at about n × 2 to the n−1 joins, 1,024 at eight tables, slide 48. Flag that this is the one 1979 decision the modern evidence overturns: with rich indexes the best bushy plan can be far cheaper, appendix slide 64. \n Source: §5: the relation being added to the join. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 76,
      "label": "51",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "3 minutes. Seven clicks, bottom-up: the singles with the keep rule; then DEPT, EMP and JOB, one per click, each box outlined in red while the panel under the lattice prices its paths; then {EMP, DEPT} and {EMP, JOB}, priced the same way; then the panel clears for the question, with the two entries it asks about red in their boxes. It is slide 47's loop run by hand. Read each panel top to bottom: the kept entries first, each with its arithmetic and in grey why it survives, then the struck ones and why they lost. Level one is slide 46's keep rule applied to all three tables, and it contains a small repeat of slide 33: DEPT's index on its own filter column costs 10.2 and loses to a five-page scan. JOB's segment scan reads its 2 pages, exactly the unique TITLE index's 1 + 1 = 2, and both are unordered; only one unordered entry is kept, so the lattice keeps the index and strikes the scan as a tie. Nothing downstream moves: a JOB-first pair through the scan would cost the same 2 + 201 = 203. EMP's SAL index, which matches nothing in this query, is a 10,040-page pass in an order nobody wants. Level two is built by extending each level-one entry with one more table, both methods, keeping the best per order. The per-lookup prices in its panels are Table 2's: 5.5 for one probe of EMP's clustered DNO index, slide 37, 201 for one probe of its non-clustered JOB index, 1/50 × (50 + 10,000), one job code's 200 scattered rows at a page each plus a fiftieth of the index, which is printed only on appendix slide 70, so say it aloud; and 2 for one lookup through a unique index. Two entries deserve a finger: EMP and DEPT in JOB order at 30,050, and EMP and JOB in DNO order at 20,550, produce the same rows as the 60 and the 203 in their own subsets, and each is kept only because it delivers an order the triple might want. Ask the room whether they will ever be used, then answer it on the next slide: no, both lose to the cheap plan plus a sort. That is the honest outcome of the interesting-order rule on this data, and it is why the rule is cheap insurance rather than a guarantee. For students who check the paper: its own example prunes EMP's segment scan and keeps JOB's, because its sizes are not ours; here EMP's scan is the cheapest path and JOB's falls only on a tie. The mechanism is the same. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5, Figures 2 to 5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
