      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "3 minutes. Seven clicks, bottom-up: the singles with the keep rule; then DEPT, EMP and JOB, one per click, each box outlined in red while the panel under the lattice prices its paths; then {EMP, DEPT} and {EMP, JOB}, priced the same way; then the panel clears for the question, with the two entries it asks about red in their boxes. It is slide 47's loop run by hand. Read each panel top to bottom: the kept entries first, each with its arithmetic and in grey why it survives, then the struck ones and why they lost. Level one is slide 46's keep rule applied to all three tables, and it contains a small repeat of slide 33: DEPT's index on its own filter column costs 10.2 and loses to a five-page scan. JOB's segment scan reads its 2 pages, exactly the unique TITLE index's 1 + 1 = 2, and both are unordered; only one unordered entry is kept, so the lattice keeps the index and strikes the scan as a tie. Nothing downstream moves: a JOB-first pair through the scan would cost the same 2 + 201 = 203. EMP's SAL index, which matches nothing in this query, is a 10,040-page pass in an order nobody wants. Level two is built by extending each level-one entry with one more table, both methods, keeping the best per order. The per-lookup prices in its panels are Table 2's: 5.5 for one probe of EMP's clustered DNO index, slide 37, 201 for one probe of its non-clustered JOB index, 1/50 × (50 + 10,000), one job code's 200 scattered rows at a page each plus a fiftieth of the index, which is printed only on appendix slide 70, so say it aloud; and 2 for one lookup through a unique index. Two entries deserve a finger: EMP and DEPT in JOB order at 30,050, and EMP and JOB in DNO order at 20,550, produce the same rows as the 60 and the 203 in their own subsets, and each is kept only because it delivers an order the triple might want. Ask the room whether they will ever be used, then answer it on the next slide: no, both lose to the cheap plan plus a sort. That is the honest outcome of the interesting-order rule on this data, and it is why the rule is cheap insurance rather than a guarantee. For students who check the paper: its own example prunes EMP's segment scan and keeps JOB's, because its sizes are not ours; here EMP's scan is the cheapest path and JOB's falls only on a tie. The mechanism is the same. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5, Figures 2 to 5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
