    {
      "idx": 83,
      "label": "52",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three clicks: the pairs from the last slide, its question gone, then the triple on top outlined in red, with the panel pricing it and the five plans it beats, then the winning path in red. The panel's last row answers the last slide's question: the two ordered entries skip a sort, but the sort they save costs only 20 or 100 pages, and they pay about 20,000 and 30,000 for it. The payoff for the whole middle of the lecture, in three beats. First, the winner uses both join methods and a sort, a nested-loop probe to build EMP and DEPT and then a merge to add JOB, and no rule book reaches it. Second, the merge wins here for exactly the reason slide 39 gave: the composite is only 1,000 rows, so sorting it costs 2 × ceil(1,000 / 20) = 100 pages and the merge's inner is one 3-page pass of JOB through its clustered index, 1 + 2, against 1,000 × 2 = 2,000 pages of probing. On the pair alone the merge lost by 557 − 60 = 497; once JOB is added, sorting wins by 2,000 − 103 = 1,897. Third, price the order the user actually wrote, run as written with lookups for the first pair: EMP outside probes DEPT for the pair at 500 + 10,000 × 2 = 20,500, then a sort and merge add JOB, 20,500 + 100 + 3 = 20,603, which is 126 times the winner. The four legal orders, with lookups for the first pair and a sort and merge for the third, are 163, 230, 20,527 and 20,603; the other two orders are cross products and were never enumerated. With a merge for the first pair, EMP first does far better, 557 + 100 + 3 = 660, which is why the search prices both methods. The best plan built on the other pair, 230, starts from JOB: 203 for the pair, a 20-page sort of its 200 rows on DNO, and one 7-page pass of DEPT. A sharp student may extend the kept 62 and 204 entries too: they only add 2 or 1 to plans already shown, 165, 2,062, 231 and 604, and their orders are no use to the last join; probing from the two ordered entries costs 32,050 and 20,950. The slide leaves these dominated plans out. The top level keeps only its cheapest plan, because the query has no ORDER BY or GROUP BY, which is why 230, in DNO order, is struck. Appendix slides 80 and 81 have every one of the 30 costs. Sort cost is our own one-pass estimate at 20 rows per page. \n Source: §5, Figure 6. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
