    {
      "idx": 86,
      "label": "53",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The whole lecture on one slide. Trace it bottom-up and name where each number came from: scan DEPT's five pages with LOC = DENVER as a search argument, 10 rows out, from slides 20 and 24; probe EMP's clustered DNO index once per Denver department, 10 × 5.5 = 55, running total 60, 1,000 rows out, from slide 37; sort those 1,000 rows on JOB, 2 × ceil(1,000 / 20) = 100 pages, running total 160, from slide 21; merge with JOB through its clustered index with TITLE = CLERK as a search argument, 1 + 2 = 3 pages, total 163, 20 rows out, from slides 39 and 40; project. EMP carries no predicate in this query, so its probe is driven only by the DNO each Denver row supplies. Then close the loop opened on slide 3 and priced on slide 9 and appendix slide 75: 163 for the plan, 22,500 for the rule book at 138 times, 20,603 for the user's own FROM order run as written, EMP outside with lookups into DEPT, at 126 times, and about 5,000,000 for the tree as parsed at roughly 31,000 times. Both strawmen are our arithmetic, not costs the paper computes. \n Source: §5, Figure 6. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 87,
      "label": "54",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The engineering answer to the question isn't this expensive. Say one more figure aloud, which is no longer on the slide: the paper puts the search for a two-way join at 5 to 20 database retrievals, a database retrieval being one row fetched through the storage layer rather than one page, which is less than the query itself costs on its first execution, while the gap between plans here is 138 times against the rule book and 126 times against the FROM order run as written, with lookups for the first pair. These are the only performance numbers in the entire paper; there is no measured plan-quality result in it, only the claim appendix slide 61 checks. Then say where this search lives now: Postgres runs the same bottom-up subset DP, bushy joins included, for fewer than geqo_threshold = 12 FROM items, then switches to a genetic search, GEQO; Cascades-style optimizers, SQL Server, Calcite, CockroachDB and Spanner among them, memoize the same subsets top-down with rules instead of bottom-up; and most engines now consider bushy plans, which 1979 did not. These are 1979 figures for an IBM 370/158, not a prediction for any current system. \n Source: §5; §7: conclusion. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 88,
      "label": "55",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The end of the core route, and no new content: one line per part of the hour, in the order it was built. Rules: each of strawman 2’s two held on one query and broke on the next, because the deciding fact was in the data, slides 12 to 16; the one rule that survives, applying filters early, is strawman 1’s pushdown, slide 10. Cost: page fetches, slide 19, the one number every plan is ranked by. Estimates: the four catalog numbers of slide 20 and Table 1’s factors, multiplied, slide 23, under the assumptions slide 26 read out, uniform values and independent columns, which is where estimates go wrong. One table: the cheapest access path, priced by Table 2, slides 30 to 33, plus the cheapest per useful order, which the search keeps, slide 46. Two tables: nested loops or merge scan, whichever costs less, slide 41. Many tables: the subset search, argued on slides 44 to 47 and run by hand on slides 51 and 52, which found the plan of slide 53. Then the result once more: 163 page fetches for the 20 rows, against 22,500 for the rule book. The three slides to re-read: 26, the assumptions; 33, where the obvious index loses to a scan by 5×; and 46, why the state carries the order. The appendix opens with the two parts this route leaves out, nested queries from slide 57 and what held up when the paper's claim was re-tested, from slide 60; the review questions are slide 85. Forward to next class: Spanner, where the cost measure grows a network term and a shard fan-out term. \n Source: §4 and §5, assembled. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 89,
      "label": "56",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 0 minutes. First the two parts the core route stops before: nested queries, slides 57 to 59, and what held up when the paper was re-tested in 2015, slides 60 to 66, ending with the whole design in eight steps. Then the cards the core route moved here because they quote costs it derives only later: the glossary and the four choice cards behind slide 3, each with its arithmetic, and the rule book's bill. Then the two tables in full, the paper's CPU term, every plan the search priced, and the follow-up evidence."
    },
    {
      "idx": 90,
      "label": "57",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 0 minutes. A predicate can contain a whole query block. Evaluate it once, or once per outer row."
    },
    {
      "idx": 91,
      "label": "58",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The paper's own example, on our EMP. The inner block references nothing from the outer block, so it is evaluated once, before the outer block runs, and its result is substituted as a constant, which means the outer predicate becomes sargable and gets a real selectivity factor, so everything from the access-path section on applies unchanged. A subquery that returns a list is handled the same way: evaluated once, and the list plugged in. When the values are not yet known at optimization time, Table 1 supplies the factor for column IN subquery: the subquery's expected result_rows over the product of rows(T) across its FROM list, which is appendix slide 77. The value 15000 is illustrative, the midpoint of the SAL keys from 5,000 to 25,000, not a computed average. \n Source: §6: nested queries; Table 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 92,
      "label": "59",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The same layout as slide 58, query on top and the same two boxes in the same positions, with one change: an arrow now carries a value from the outer row into the inner block, X.DNO, so the inner block is re-evaluated per outer row and the badge reads outer_rows times instead of once. The query asks for the employees who earn more than their own department's average; the paper's own example asks for those who earn more than their manager, a column our EMP does not have, and the shape is identical. This is rule 2’s lookups again, slide 37, with the subquery as the inner: a nested-loop join wearing a different syntax. Name the one optimization the paper does offer, which is not on the slide, so say it: if the referenced column repeats across outer rows, reuse the previous result, and sort the outer on that column if the saving pays; the paper's cue for whether it pays is rows(T) greater than distinct(I) on an index over the referenced column. Here rows(EMP) is 10,000 against distinct(DNO) of 100, and EMP is already stored in DNO order, so reading it through its clustered DNO index delivers each department's 100 employees together with no sort, and the average is computed 100 times instead of 10,000. Then the honest limitation, which is the real lesson: because System R keeps each subquery as its own block and treats it as a procedure rather than a join, the dynamic programming of the many-tables part never sees across the boundary, so no join order is considered there at all. System R stops there; rewriting correlated subqueries into joins, unnesting, came with later systems and closes that gap, and the gap is why a correlated subquery could be catastrophically slow in early systems. \n Source: §6: correlation subqueries. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 93,
      "label": "60",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 0 minutes. Thirty-six years later, someone ran the experiment the paper promised. Check each of the three things we had to invent on slide 17, the cost measure, the statistics and the search, against the result."
    },
    {
      "idx": 94,
      "label": "61",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The paper's own check, in one line: absolute costs often wrong, the ranking of plans usually right. The re-run is Leis et al., VLDB 2015, and its 2018 journal version. This is the fair standard to judge the paper by, and it is narrower than it is usually quoted: three distinct claims, that the absolute numbers are often wrong, that the best plan is chosen in a large majority of cases, and that only in many cases is the whole ordering identical to the measured ordering. The paper also describes these as preliminary results from evaluation work still in progress, and the promised validation arrived thirty-six years later from someone else. Say why Leis built a new benchmark rather than using TPC-H, in their words: the synthetic generators use the very same simplifying assumptions, uniformity, independence and the principle of inclusion, that query optimizers make, which trivializes cardinality estimation. The specifics: 629 base-table selections, over 100,000 intermediate estimates, measured on PostgreSQL, HyPer and three anonymised commercial engines, with cardinalities extracted from each and injected into one engine so a single executor runs every plan. A ranking tool, not a predictor. Reference: #link(\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol9/p204-leis.pdf\")[https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol9/p204-leis.pdf] and #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf] \n Source: §7: conclusion. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 95,
      "label": "62",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The slide that judges 1979’s statistics, and the vindication of slide 26. Single-table selectivity is essentially solved, with median q-error 1.00 for PostgreSQL, 1.02 for HyPer and 1.06 at worst across the systems tested, which is Table 1 doing fine, and the two best estimators get there with a table sample rather than a histogram. Joins are where it collapses, and the authors name the mechanism: systematic underestimation due to applying the independence assumption, with the conclusion that no system tested was able to detect join-crossing correlations. That is the LOC and ZIP picture of slide 26, paid once per join: each join multiplies another factor in, and the errors compound. Then two honest caveats, because the slogan is not the result. First, the direction is workload-dependent: the authors' own 2025 retrospective reports overestimation on a different benchmark, so this is underestimation on workloads like the 113 IMDB queries, not a law. Second, independence is the principal mechanism but not the sole cause: when they replaced PostgreSQL's underestimated distinct counts with exact ones, underestimation got worse, because two wrong assumptions had been partly cancelling. For the exponential framing, Ioannidis and Christodoulakis proved in 1991 that estimation error grows exponentially with the number of joins in the worst case under their model's assumptions, and their own paper exhibits a regime where it decreases, so that is a worst-case model result, not a claim about every query. DBMS A, B and C are anonymised commercial systems; no per-system number here names a product. Reference: #link(\"https://www.csd.uoc.gr/~hy460/pdf/p268-ioannidis.pdf\")[https://www.csd.uoc.gr/~hy460/pdf/p268-ioannidis.pdf] \n Source: Leis 2015, §3, Table 1 and Figure 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 96,
      "label": "63",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The cost measure of slide 19 gets a surprisingly good grade: crude is fine, and tuning the model's constants, such as how much CPU work counts against a page read, was never the main problem. Give Lohman's 2014 line, which is the memorable form of the whole finding: the cost model may introduce errors of at most 30% for a given cardinality, but the cardinality model can quite easily introduce errors of many orders of magnitude. Then walk it back honestly, which is what the journal version does: on disk, with a realistic random-to-sequential ratio, even perfect estimates leave a join-order optimizer picking plans about an order of magnitude off, so a good cost model matters more there. The ranking survives, because plan quality will generally still be dominated by the errors in cardinality estimation, but the gap narrows. Then the line under the table, and be exact about what it measures, because it is a different number from the table: with true cardinalities, the plans each model picks were run, and in geometric mean over all queries the tuned model's plans ran 41% faster than the default's, and the plans of a model that does nothing but count the rows passing through each operator, no I/O at all, ran 34% faster. So the trivial model recovers most of what tuning buys, and the whole gain is dwarfed by what true cardinalities buy. A main-memory result, not a universal one. Reference: #link(\"http://wp.sigmod.org/?p=1075\")[http://wp.sigmod.org/?p=1075] \n Source: Leis 2015, §5; journal version §8. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 97,
      "label": "64",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Two clicks: the three trees and the question, then the answer. The one place the 1979 design loses outright, and the slide exists so the lecture does not overclaim. The restriction under test is the left-deep heuristic of slide 50: the inner is always a base table, so two composites are never joined. Be careful here, because the literature contains a withdrawn number: the authors' 2025 retrospective states that the conference version's plan-enumeration results were incorrect due to a data-handling issue and were corrected in the journal version. The conference table priced the left-deep restriction at 1.06 times median and 4.50 times max, harmless, and exactly the figure that would have made \"the 1979 search survived\" look true. The corrected journal figure is a median of 1.94 times and up to 1,252 times once foreign-key indexes exist. Use the journal's numbers and say why. Then the deeper finding behind the condition in the subtitle, PK+FK indexes: good plans get rarer as the physical design gets richer, since the fraction of random plans within 1.5 times of optimal falls from 44% with no indexes to 39% with primary keys to 4% with foreign-key indexes, and the worst-to-best ratio rises from 101 times to 48,120 times. That is the point of slide 34 from the other end: the physical design moves the answer, and the richer it is, the harder optimization becomes. The other half of the verdict, that exhaustive enumeration still beats the heuristics in the tail, is appendix slide 82. Reference: #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf] and #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35880/35880.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35880/35880.pdf] \n Source: Leis, VLDB Journal 27(5), 2018, Table 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 98,
      "label": "64",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Two clicks: the three trees and the question, then the answer. The one place the 1979 design loses outright, and the slide exists so the lecture does not overclaim. The restriction under test is the left-deep heuristic of slide 50: the inner is always a base table, so two composites are never joined. Be careful here, because the literature contains a withdrawn number: the authors' 2025 retrospective states that the conference version's plan-enumeration results were incorrect due to a data-handling issue and were corrected in the journal version. The conference table priced the left-deep restriction at 1.06 times median and 4.50 times max, harmless, and exactly the figure that would have made \"the 1979 search survived\" look true. The corrected journal figure is a median of 1.94 times and up to 1,252 times once foreign-key indexes exist. Use the journal's numbers and say why. Then the deeper finding behind the condition in the subtitle, PK+FK indexes: good plans get rarer as the physical design gets richer, since the fraction of random plans within 1.5 times of optimal falls from 44% with no indexes to 39% with primary keys to 4% with foreign-key indexes, and the worst-to-best ratio rises from 101 times to 48,120 times. That is the point of slide 34 from the other end: the physical design moves the answer, and the richer it is, the harder optimization becomes. The other half of the verdict, that exhaustive enumeration still beats the heuristics in the tail, is appendix slide 82. Reference: #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf] and #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35880/35880.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35880/35880.pdf] \n Source: Leis, VLDB Journal 27(5), 2018, Table 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 99,
      "label": "65",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. Earn each clause from the slides behind it. The subset search and interesting orders are intact and vindicated: appendix slide 82 prices them against three heuristics, and the authors' lesson is that in rich schemas with foreign-key indexes, exhaustive plan enumeration provides tangible benefits. Left-deep-only is the one restriction that had to go, which slide 64 priced. The four catalog statistics and the 1/10 and 1/3 constants were replaced by histograms and samples, which slide 62 showed working. And uniformity and independence, which Selinger wrote down in one line each, were still in force in every system Leis measured. The short form you will hear, the 1979 search survived and the 1979 statistics did not, is right about the enumeration; the corrected journal table adds that the space it enumerated, left-deep only, had to widen, which is why bushy plans are on the notes' list of what 1979 did not have, appendix slide 84. Selinger's candour is the model to copy, because both assumptions are printed in Table 1, in plain sight. The 2015 conference version's plan-enumeration tables were withdrawn; these are the 2018 journal figures. \n Source: §4, §7; Leis 2015 §3; journal 2018 Tables 3 and 4. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 100,
      "label": "66",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. No new content: the whole optimizer in the order a query meets it, eight steps, each one built in the core route or the two parts just before this slide. Walk it top to bottom once and point at where each step came from. One, rewrite and pushdown: strawman 1’s fix, slide 10. System R's rewriting is that small, conjunctive normal form and each boolean factor applied as early as possible; expanding views and flattening or unnesting subqueries came with later systems, and System R keeps each subquery as its own block. Two, the cost measure: page fetches, slide 19. The paper adds a CPU term we leave out; it is appendix slide 79, for anyone who asks. Three, the four catalog numbers, refreshed by a manual UPDATE STATISTICS: slide 20. Four, one selectivity factor per boolean factor from Table 1, multiplied: slide 23, and the multiplying is what slide 62 just indicted. Five, per table the cheapest access path overall and the cheapest per useful order: slide 46. Six, nested loops or merge scan, whichever costs less, never a rule: slide 41. Seven, the subset search: slides 51 and 52, which found 163 page fetches for the 20 rows. Eight, nested queries: slides 58 and 59. Steps two to four are the cost measure and the statistics, and five to seven the search, so this is slide 17’s three inventions, built. Then the three slides to re-read: 33, where the obvious index loses by 5×; 46, why the state carries the order; and 64, what the 1979 space left out. The review questions are appendix slide 85. \n Source: §3–§6, assembled; discussion based on §7. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 101,
      "label": "67",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The glossary the next four cards need, for anyone who jumps here from slide 3 before the core route has earned the words. Read each line once and move on; every term has arithmetic under it in the core route. Page fetch is the unit for the whole hour; slide 19's note names the paper's second, CPU term and drops it, and slide 79 has the rest. Access path, segment scan, clustered and matching are the single-table vocabulary of slides 29 to 34; segment scan is only the paper's name for a full scan, every page of the table once. Legal order is the no-cross-product heuristic of slide 49, stated here because the very next card prices four orders and calls them legal; say the reason out loud, that two of the six orders would have to start by pairing every DEPT row with every JOB row, because those two tables share no column. Nested loops and merge scan are the two join methods of slides 36 to 41: nested loops are rule 2's lookups, merge scan is the merge join of slides 39 and 40. If the room already has the words, skip the card: the four after it are the ones carrying numbers. \n Source: §3, §4, §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 102,
      "label": "68",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The first of four cards, one per question on slide 3: the choice, the competing plans, and the arithmetic that prices them. All four legal orders are priced here so the spread is not taken on faith. Two of the six orders, DEPT then JOB and JOB then DEPT, are cross products, because those two tables share no column, and the optimizer never enumerates them. Read each sum left to right: read the first table, look the second up once per surviving row, sort, then merge the third. The first term is how each table is read: DEPT by a five-page scan, EMP by a 500-page scan, and JOB through its unique TITLE index, one index page and one data page, 1 + 1 = 2. That 2 is the TITLE index, not a scan, though a segment scan of JOB's two pages also costs 2: a tie, and the search keeps the index, slide 80; either way the JOB-first sum is 230. Almost all of the spread is the first table's surviving row count, 10 Denver departments against 1 clerk job against all 10,000 employees, since that count multiplies everything above it. EMP keeps all 10,000 because the paper's WHERE clause does not filter it at all, which is exactly where rule 2 breaks, slide 16. JOB first has the smallest count of all and still loses to DEPT first, 230 against 163, because the only way into EMP on JOB is a non-clustered index at 201 pages a lookup. The spread is 20,603 / 163 = 126.4, and the FROM list as written, EMP, DEPT, JOB, is the worst of the four, run the way this card runs every order: the first pair joined by lookups. Every number comes off the statistics on slide 21; 163 and 230 are priced on slide 52's panel and 20,603 on slide 53. If someone checks this against slide 81, the bottom two rows are not in it: the card fixes the method, lookups for the first pair, so that only the order varies, and both rows are built on the 20,500 pair plan that the search prunes before it ever reaches the triple. That is the point of slide 45, arriving early. If someone asks why EMP first could not merge with DEPT instead, it could, at 557, and that plan is on slide 73; with the sort and the merge for JOB on top it costs 557 + 100 + 3 = 660, still about four times 163. So 20,603 is the FROM order run with lookups, not EMP first at its own best. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 103,
      "label": "69",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The winning sum from the previous card, unfolded, because a four-term expression is not self-explanatory however carefully it is laid out. Read the terms aloud in order. Five is a segment scan of DEPT, all five of its pages, with LOC = 'DENVER' tested as a search argument, and ten of its hundred rows survive, from the 1/10 on slide 24. Then ten lookups into EMP, one per surviving Denver department, each costing one hundredth of EMP's DNO path: fifty index pages plus five hundred data pages is 550, and one DNO value in a hundred is 5.5. Say out loud that 5.5 is an expected cost and not half a page fetch, because the optimizer works in expectations throughout. Those first two terms are exactly the 60 on slide 73, and the 1,000 pairs they produce are the input to the last two: each Denver department brings all 100 of its employees, because nothing filters EMP. A hundred sorts those 1,000 rows into JOB order at twenty rows a page, out and back, 2 × ceil(1,000 / 20), which is this lecture's convention and not the paper's. Three is one pass of JOB through its clustered index, one index page and two data pages, with TITLE = 'CLERK' tested as a search argument, because a merge reads each side once; 20 rows come out. No new machinery: this is the two cost formulas of slides 37 and 41 applied once each, and slide 52 re-derives the whole plan inside the search. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing join order. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 104,
      "label": "70",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The card that stops 5.5 from being a magic number, and it pays for itself three times over, because these three lookup costs carry every join cost in the deck. A lookup is not a new formula: it is Table 2 again, on the inner table, with the outer row's value supplied as the constant, which is what makes the join predicate sargable and lets the inner index match. So the cost is a fraction of the inner's whole access path, and the fraction is the join predicate's selectivity factor, one over the column's distinct values. Walk row one: EMP has 10,000 rows and 100 departments, so one department is 100 rows; EMP is stored in DNO order, so those 100 rows sit together on five pages at twenty rows a page, and the index contributes one hundredth of its fifty pages, half a page. Five and a half. Walk row two and let the contrast land: one job code is 200 rows, the JOB index is not the order EMP is stored in, so those 200 rows sit on 200 different pages and the deck charges a fetch for each. That is pages against rows, the whole argument of slide 32. Row three is the degenerate case, a unique index: one key, one row, one index page and one data page. These are strawman 2's rough counts made exact: about 5 reads for one department and about 200 for one job code, slide 13. Say that 5.5 is an expected cost and not half a fetch: ten lookups cost 55. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §4, Table 2; §5: computing the cost of joins. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 105,
      "label": "71",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. Why the four sums on slide 68 come in only two shapes. The last two terms do not depend on the join order as such, only on which pair was built first: DEPT with EMP leaves JOB to add, and JOB with EMP leaves DEPT. Give the two composites, 1,000 rows for EMP and DEPT and 200 rows for EMP and JOB, both of them products of slide 24's factors, and note that the second is smaller because one job title is CLERK while ten departments are in Denver. Then the two tails, a sort plus one pass, with the sort cost from this lecture's own convention and the pass being the table's clustered index pages plus its data pages. Ask why the third table is not simply looked up once per composite row, and answer with slide 74: 1,000 lookups of a unique index cost 2,000 against 103 for a sort and a merge, and 200 lookups cost 400 against 27. Then flag what this slide quietly makes concrete, because it is the second half of the lecture in miniature: the tail is the same number whatever the pair below it cost, which is the exchange argument on slide 45 and the reason the search can keep one plan per subset. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing join order; §5: n-way joins. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 106,
      "label": "72",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The second card, and the one place the cards leave the running query. The paper's WHERE clause never filters EMP, so on our query every EMP index path reads the whole index and the whole table, and nothing can match; slide 80 prices those four at 500, 550, 10,050 and 10,040. To see the index-or-scan choice at all, take the one-table query that rule 1 and the access-path section use, SAL > 20000 on EMP. One predicate, four paths, the same 2,500 rows found by every one of them, so the comparison is honest. The scan reads all 500 pages and tests SAL as a search argument on the way past, so the rows that fail cost no extra page; that is Table 2's segment-scan row, pages(EMP) = 500. The two non-matching index scans read a whole index and then the whole table, which is why they are dear; on the running query they are kept anyway, because they deliver DNO order and JOB order, free for the join above, slide 46. The matching index is the one rule 1 picks, and it is the one that loses: EMP is stored in DNO order, a table has only one stored order, so the SAL index is non-clustered and every one of the 2,500 qualifying rows costs its own page fetch. That substitution of rows for pages is the whole story, derived on slides 32 to 34. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 107,
      "label": "73",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The third card, and the one that needs the reversal said out loud. Both methods cost the outer path plus one inner pass per surviving outer row, so the whole contest is what one inner pass costs. Nested loops are rule 2's lookups; merge scan is the merge join of slide 39. On this pair the merge loses, and not because merging is slow: its outer input, EMP in DNO order, costs 550 by itself before any merging starts, while a DEPT scan costs five. EMP outside with lookups is worse still, 20,500, because all 10,000 EMP rows look DEPT up; that is 342 times the 60. One level up the same comparison flips, because the composite is only 1,000 rows: sorting it costs 100 and the merge's inner is one 3-page pass of JOB, 1 index page and 2 data pages, against 1,000 lookups of two pages each, 2,000. So the merge loses by 557 − 60 = 497 on the pair and wins by 2,000 − 103 = 1,897 on the triple, and the answer to which join method is neither, until the data is known. Derived on slides 36 to 41, with the reversal on slide 52 and every pair plan on slide 81. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §5: computing the cost of joins. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 108,
      "label": "74",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The fourth card. A sort is never free and is sometimes the cheapest thing on the slide, so the question is only ever what the resulting order is worth above it. Row one is the winning plan's sort and it is the best bargain in the lecture: 100 pages turn 2,000 pages of JOB lookups into a 3-page merge. Row two is the same trick on the pair of the other branch, 20 pages that save 373. Row three is the same trick refusing to pay: 10,000 rows cost 1,000 to sort on top of the 500-page scan, while reading EMP through its clustered DNO index arrives sorted for 50 more than the scan, so sorting loses by 950 and the ordered path is kept instead. That is slide 46's comparison: EMP comes in DNO order through its clustered DNO index for 550, the 500 data pages plus the index's own 50. A plain segment scan promises no order at all, so the ordered path is the index, never the scan. Nothing about the operation changed between row one and row three; only the row count did. Note the convention, because it is ours and not the paper's: sorting Q rows costs two passes at 20 rows per page, one out and one back, and the paper gives no sort-production formula at all. Derived on slides 46 and 52. Illustrative costs in page fetches, not measurements. \n Source: §4: interesting order; §5: merging scans. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 109,
      "label": "75",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Two clicks: the two plan shapes with every cost held back, then the costs and the verdict. The rule book from slide 12, priced on the paper's own query; it sits here because it quotes costs the core route derives only later. Show the two plan shapes and make the room commit to a guess before the reveal. The rules, as worded on slide 12: always use an index if one exists; always join in FROM order. FROM order puts EMP first, and the paper's WHERE clause gives EMP no predicate an index could match, so it is scanned: 500 pages, 10,000 rows. Rule 1 then fires on each join. Every EMP row looks DEPT up through its unique DNO index, 10,000 × 2 = 20,000, running total 20,500, and 1,000 pairs survive LOC. Each pair looks JOB up through its unique index, 1,000 × 2 = 2,000, total 22,500, and 20 rows survive TITLE. The winner is 163, so 22,500 / 163 = 138. The rule book is not stupid, every individual rule is defensible, and it still loses by two orders of magnitude. The winning plan on the right is derived on slides 51 to 53; do not explain it here. Our arithmetic for both plans, not a cost the paper computes. \n Source: §4, §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 110,
      "label": "75",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Two clicks: the two plan shapes with every cost held back, then the costs and the verdict. The rule book from slide 12, priced on the paper's own query; it sits here because it quotes costs the core route derives only later. Show the two plan shapes and make the room commit to a guess before the reveal. The rules, as worded on slide 12: always use an index if one exists; always join in FROM order. FROM order puts EMP first, and the paper's WHERE clause gives EMP no predicate an index could match, so it is scanned: 500 pages, 10,000 rows. Rule 1 then fires on each join. Every EMP row looks DEPT up through its unique DNO index, 10,000 × 2 = 20,000, running total 20,500, and 1,000 pairs survive LOC. Each pair looks JOB up through its unique index, 1,000 × 2 = 2,000, total 22,500, and 20 rows survive TITLE. The winner is 163, so 22,500 / 163 = 138. The rule book is not stupid, every individual rule is defensible, and it still loses by two orders of magnitude. The winning plan on the right is derived on slides 51 to 53; do not explain it here. Our arithmetic for both plans, not a cost the paper computes. \n Source: §4, §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 111,
      "label": "76",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The left column of the previous figure, unfolded exactly as slide 69 unfolded the right one, and worth the minute because the figure's labels are running totals: the 20,500 inside 22,500 is not a new cost, it is the step below being carried up. Read the three terms. First, 500 reads EMP by a segment scan: rule 2 puts EMP first, and nothing in the paper's WHERE clause filters EMP, so rule 1 has no predicate for an index to match, and all 10,000 rows come out. Second, those 10,000 rows each drive one lookup of DEPT on DNO, a unique index at one index page plus one data page, so 20,000. Say the sting out loud: only 1,000 of those 10,000 lookups find a Denver department, and all 10,000 are paid for, because the lookup is driven by the outer row's DNO value and LOC can only be tested once the DEPT row is in hand. DEPT does have a LOC index, but it is on the wrong column for this lookup. Third, the 1,000 pairs each look JOB up on its unique index, another 2 apiece, 2,000 in all, and 20 rows come out. Then the decomposition, which is the real payoff: the FROM order adds 20,500 − 60 = 20,440 on the first pair, the same 20,440 slide 45 uses; looking JOB up instead of sorting the composite and merging adds 2,000 − 103 = 1,897, the saving slide 74 priced. Those two add to 22,337, which is exactly 22,500 − 163, so the gap is fully accounted for and nothing is hand-waved. Note what is missing: rule 1 costs nothing extra on this query, because EMP has no predicate for it to misuse; its breaking case is the one-table query of slide 14 and slide 72. Our arithmetic, not a cost the paper computes. \n Source: §4, §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 112,
      "label": "77",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The four constants are 1/10 for an equality without an index, 1/4 for BETWEEN, 1/3 for an open-ended range, and 1/2 as both the IN cap and the ceiling the others sit under. These are the rows the core route abridged on slide 23, and the only place the paper shows a derivation. The range row has two conditions: the column must be arithmetic, and the value must be known when the plan is chosen; a host variable in a compiled program is not, so the 1/3 guess is used instead of interpolation. For column1 = column2 with an index on only one side, the paper uses one over that index's distinct values; with no index on either side, 1/10. For columnA IN subquery: assume the subquery column's values contain the outer column's values, so if the whole subquery table is selected the predicate is always true and F = 1; if the subquery is restricted, assume the matching values are restricted in proportion, so F is the subquery's own fraction, its result rows over the product of its tables' rows. The paper then extends this to subqueries that are joins and to arithmetic expressions with a little optimism, its words. That containment assumption is the principle of inclusion, which Leis names as one of the three assumptions still in force. The paper prints ICARD where this slide says distinct, and QCARD and NCARD in the subquery row; slide 20 has the mapping. \n Source: §4, Table 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 113,
      "label": "78",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The third panel of the slide 31 and 32 pair, and the honest footnote to slide 33. The alternative charges pages instead of rows on the grounds that the fetched pages stay resident, which is literally the same substitution that makes a clustered index cheap, so the buffer-fit non-clustered index behaves like a clustered one: 1/4 × (40 + 500) = 135, which now beats the 500-page scan. Do not sharpen the condition: the paper prints if this number fits in the System R buffer, and the surrounding prose speaks of whether the set of tuples retrieved will fit entirely in the RSS buffer pool, or effective buffer pool per user; the RSS is System R's storage layer, pictured on slide 79. Note that Table 2 gives no buffer-fit alternative for clustered rows, because there is nothing to gain, and that the decision rests on a buffer size the optimizer knows only approximately. On our statistics it also flips a core-deck pruning: DEPT's LOC index costs 1/10 × (2 + 5) = 0.7 under the buffer-fit formula and would beat the five-page scan. The core deck uses the no-fit formulas everywhere so that there is one rule. \n Source: §4, Table 2 and its accompanying paragraph. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 114,
      "label": "79",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The paper's full cost formula, for anyone who reads the paper: slide 19's approximate sign stands for the second term, which its note names and drops, and this card is the rest of the story. System R has two layers. Below the line, the RSS, the Research Storage System, keeps the tables and indexes on disk. Above it, the compiled plan runs the joins, the sorts, and any predicate the RSS cannot test itself. The plan never touches a page: it opens a scan through the RSS's interface, the RSI, and calls NEXT, and each call passes one row up; that crossing, in red, is one RSI call. Point at the RSS box: one call can do a lot of work below the line. The RSS reads pages as needed and tests each row against the search arguments of slide 29, skipping rows that fail until one passes, so a rejected row costs page reads but no call. Why count calls and not instructions: instructions are hard to predict before the plan runs, rows passed up are easy, because selectivity gives them; and the paper's argument that the count is a fair stand-in is that most of System R's CPU time is spent inside the RSS. W is the exchange rate that lets the two terms be added: one call costs W page fetches. Do it with made-up timings: a page fetch of 10 ms and a call of 0.1 ms make W = 0.1 / 10 = 0.01, so 100 calls cost one page. The one-table query SAL > 20000 then costs 500 + 2,500 × 0.01 = 500 + 25 = 525 by a scan, 2,500 calls for the 2,500 rows it passes up, and 500 of the 525 is I/O. W matters only when one plan saves I/O by spending CPU, so take two made-up plans: A reads 100 pages and makes 10,000 calls, B reads 150 pages and makes 1,000. At W = 0.01, A costs 100 + 100 = 200 and B costs 150 + 10 = 160, so B wins. Make the CPU ten times faster, W = 0.001: A costs 100 + 10 = 110 and B costs 150 + 1 = 151, so A wins. Same query, same data, same two plans; only the machine changed. So W belongs to the machine, not the query: a setting, not something the optimizer computes. PostgreSQL's cpu_tuple_cost is the same knob, default 0.01 of a sequential page fetch, whose own cost, seq_page_cost, is 1.0. Then why the deck counts pages only: the paper gives W no value, and on one table every path applies the same search arguments and passes up the same rows, so W × calls is the same on each and cannot change the winner. Between join plans the call counts do differ, and we drop the term anyway, for want of a W to put in it. The timings and both plans are made up to show the arithmetic. \n Source: §3: the RSS, the RSI and search arguments; §4: the cost formula and the weighting factor W; PostgreSQL documentation, cpu_tuple_cost. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 115,
      "label": "80",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. For students who want to redo the deck with a calculator. Ten paths, three tables, every cost from slide 21. On the running query EMP has no predicate, so none of its indexes matches anything and each index path reads the whole index and then the whole table: the clustered DNO index 50 + 500, the non-clustered JOB and SAL indexes one fetch per row, 50 + 10,000 and 40 + 10,000. The DNO and JOB paths are kept for their orders, which the joins want; SAL order is wanted by nothing, so 10,040 is pruned. DEPT's scan tests LOC as a search argument and JOB's clustered index tests TITLE the same way. Note the small repeat of slide 33 here: DEPT's index on its own filter column costs 10.2 and loses to a five-page scan. JOB's segment scan reads its two pages for 2, the same as the TITLE index: a tie between two unordered paths, so the search keeps one, the TITLE index, and strikes the scan; a JOB-first plan through the scan would cost the same 2 + 201 = 203, so nothing above it changes. Reconstruct the crossing rule once more on the one-table query: a non-clustered index beats the scan exactly when `F × (index_pages + rows)` is less than `pages`, so for EMP's SAL index whenever fewer than about 498 of its 10,000 rows qualify, slide 34. All illustrative: the paper computes no costs anywhere. \n Source: §4, Table 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 116,
      "label": "81",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The twenty costs behind slides 51 and 52. Each pair prices seven plans and keeps three: the cheapest unordered and the cheapest in each interesting order. Point at the candidates slide 51's panels priced two to a line: the EMP-JOB merge through EMP's JOB index, 10,050 + 3 = 10,053, and the EMP-JOB plan with EMP outside through its JOB index, 10,050 + 10,000 × 2 = 30,050; the EMP-DEPT merge that scans EMP and sorts it, 500 + 1,000 + 7 = 1,507, is the one slide 46 derives. The two dear kept entries, 30,050 and 20,550, exist only because of their order: they produce the same rows as the 60 and the 203 in their own subsets, and the triple discards both, at 30,053 and 20,557. Every nested-loop pair cost is the outer path's cost from slide 80 plus `outer_rows` times one inner lookup: 5.5 pages through EMP's clustered DNO index, 201 through its non-clustered JOB index, 2 through either unique index. The four merges instead cost an ordered EMP plus one pass of the inner, 7 for DEPT or 3 for JOB, where the ordered EMP is 550 through the DNO index, 10,050 through the JOB index, or the 500 scan plus 1,000 to sort it. {DEPT, JOB} is never entered: it has no join predicate. Sort cost is our own convention, 2 × ceil(Q / 20), out and back at 20 rows per page. All illustrative: the paper computes no costs anywhere. \n Source: §5. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 117,
      "label": "82",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The honest version of \"dynamic programming still beats heuristics\". With primary-key indexes only, a greedy heuristic edges it at the median, 1.01 against 1.03, because with wrong estimates an exhaustive search optimizes the wrong objective more thoroughly; its advantage is in the tails and in rich physical designs. The authors' own register is moderate but not insignificant, and they immediately add that the performance potential from good cardinality estimates is certainly much larger. The real argument for keeping the exhaustive search is that it is cheap, since exhaustive algorithms solve dozens of tables quickly, so there is no reason to give up the tail protection. Note too that index-awareness matters as much as exhaustiveness: the two heuristics that consider indexes beat the one that ignores them. The 2015 conference version's plan-enumeration tables were withdrawn; these are the 2018 journal figures. DBMS A, B and C are anonymised commercial systems; no per-system number here names a product. Reference: #link(\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf\")[https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27562/27562.pdf] \n Source: Leis, VLDB Journal 27(5), 2018, Table 4. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 118,
      "label": "83",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. The implementation detail that keeps the search table from blowing up on long join chains. Because the number of stored solutions is 2 to the n times the number of interesting orders, collapsing syntactically distinct join columns into one class shrinks the table directly, and one saved plan then covers the whole class. F here is a third, hypothetical table; the running query has two classes, EMP.DNO with DEPT.DNO and EMP.JOB with JOB.JOB. \n Source: §5: equivalence classes. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 119,
      "label": "84",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. The lecture notes' list, with dates: hash join, histograms, bushy plans, parallel plans, re-optimizing when estimates turn out wrong, and plan caching that notices data changed. Sampling is the one addition worth naming aloud, since it is now the usual basis for statistics: Lipton, Naughton and Schneider, 1990. The gap between a good idea and a shipped feature is decades: bushy enumeration reached one major commercial optimizer about thirty-five years after Selinger. LEO's negative lesson is the best material here, because learning true cardinalities from execution feedback can make the plans you did not run look better than they are, since they are still estimated by an underestimating model; the retrospective calls it fleeing from knowledge to ignorance. What shipped widely is narrower and operator-level: switching a join algorithm or a distribution strategy at runtime. The last row is the notes' plan caching that notices data changed: System R did precompile and store access modules, described by Chamberlin et al. in 1981, but this deck claims nothing about how a stored plan was invalidated when the catalog changed, because that mechanism is not sourced here; the row gives only a shipped example, PostgreSQL 8.3, whose release notes list automatically re-planning cached queries when table definitions change or statistics are updated (#link(\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/release/8.3.0/\")[https://www.postgresql.org/docs/release/8.3.0/]). Research dates are first publication, not adoption. Postgres 8.3 and 9.6 are the releases that shipped the feature; 9.4 is different, only the release Leis 2015 tested, and Postgres's subset search considered bushy joins well before it, so read \"by 9.4\" as \"at the latest\". Hybrid hash is DeWitt and colleagues, SIGMOD 1984; Shapiro's 1986 TODS paper is the later analysis. \n Source: §5, §7; Leis 2015 §2; Ioannidis, VLDB 2003; PostgreSQL 8.3 release notes. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    },
    {
      "idx": 120,
      "label": "85",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. Five of the thirteen questions in the lecture notes; use them as an exit ticket or the first five minutes of the next class. Question 5 draws on the appendix's 2015 re-run, slides 61 to 65 and 82, so skip it if the class stopped at the recap. Answers. One: the one-table query SAL > 20000 on EMP; the two statistics are pages and rows, 500 against 10,000, because the scattered SAL index pays a fetch per qualifying row, 1/4 × (40 + 10,000) = 2,510, while the scan pays per page, 500; slides 14 and 33. Two: pages becomes rows; clustered rows share pages, so each page is fetched once, while a non-clustered index's neighbouring entries point at different pages, so each row costs a fetch and a page can come back many times; the buffer-fit exception is slide 78. Three: the exchange argument of slide 45, since adding JOB costs the same whatever built the {EMP, DEPT} composite, so every total falls by the same 20,440 when the 60 plan replaces the 20,500 one; it needs per interesting order because a dearer plan can deliver an order the next merge wants, 557 against 1,507 on slide 46. Four: an underestimate, tenfold on slide 26's table, because the ZIP factor rejects nobody the LOC factor kept, so multiplying divides by ten twice where reality divides once. Five: the enumeration survived, though its left-deep space had to widen, slide 64, and the statistics did not; with bad estimates DP still avoids the catastrophic plans that heuristics find, and it is cheap, slide 82. The other eight, briefly. Every output row satisfies every boolean factor, so a factor can run as early as you like, and pushdown killed strawman 1. Cost is counted in pages because a disk read brings a whole page, so rows matter only through the pages they touch. Counted in rows, rule 1 would always look right, 2,500 rows found by the SAL index against 10,000 read by the scan; counted in pages, the scattered index pays about a page per row, 2,510 against 500, slides 19 and 32. A sargable predicate has the form column op value: a scan tests it as it reads, so failing rows cost no extra page, and an index whose key starts with that column uses it to start and stop its range; column = column inside one table is not sargable, slide 29. An interesting order is one that ORDER BY, GROUP BY or a join column wants, kept because fifty pages can save 950, slide 46. Nested loops and merge scan share `C = C_outer + outer_rows × C_inner`, and merge wins when one pass is cheaper than many lookups, 100 + 3 against 2,000. Left-deep rules out joining two composites, and buys index lookups on a base-table inner, pipelining and a smaller search. A cross product is unavoidable only when the join graph is disconnected. A correlated subquery is secretly a nested-loop join with the outer block as the outer. \n Source: Discussion based on §4–§6. https://doi.org/10.1145/582095.582099"
    }
  ]
}
